./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:2:benchmark:10,CellPLM: Pre-training of Cell Language Model Beyond Single Cells,"The current state-of-the-art single-cell pre-trained models are greatly inspired by the success of large language models. They trained transformers by treating genes as tokens and cells as sentences. However, three fundamental differences between single-cell data and natural language data are overlooked: (1) scRNA-seq data are presented as bag-of-genes instead of sequences of RNAs; (2) Cell-cell relations are more intricate and important than inter-sentence relations; and (3) The quantity of single-cell data is considerably inferior to text data, and they are very noisy. In light of these characteristics, we propose a new pre-trained model CellPLM , which takes cells as tokens and tissues as sentences. In addition, we leverage spatially-resolved transcriptomic data in pre-training to facilitate learning cell-cell relationships and introduce a Gaussian mixture prior distribution as an additional inductive bias to overcome data limitation. CellPLM is the first single-cell pre-trained transformer that encodes cell-cell relations and it consistently outperforms existing pre-trained and non-pre-trained models in diverse downstream tasks, with 100x times higher inference speed compared to existing pre-trained models.",benchmark_boundary_case,CellPLM: Pre-training of Cell Language Model Beyond Single Cells,"The current state-of-the-art single-cell pre-trained models are greatly inspired by the success of large language models. They trained transformers by treating genes as tokens and cells as sentences. However, three fundamental differences between single-cell data and natural language data are overlooked: (1) scRNA-seq data are presented as bag-of-genes instead of sequences of RNAs; (2) Cell-cell relations are more intricate and important than inter-sentence relations; and (3) The quantity of single-cell data is considerably inferior to text data, and they are very noisy. In light of these characteristics, we propose a new pre-trained model CellPLM , which takes cells as tokens and tissues as sentences. In addition, we leverage spatially-resolved transcriptomic data in pre-training to facilitate learning cell-cell relationships and introduce a Gaussian mixture prior distribution as an additional inductive bias to overcome data limitation. CellPLM is the first single-cell pre-trained transformer that encodes cell-cell relations and it consistently outperforms existing pre-trained and non-pre-trained models in diverse downstream tasks, with 100x times higher inference speed compared to existing pre-trained models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"The abstract describes CellPLM as a pre-trained transformer that treats cells as tokens and tissues as sentences, which is biological-token modeling without any natural-language text component; thus it fails the text_component criterion. | The abstract describes a pre-trained transformer for single-cell data (CellPLM) that encodes cell-cell relations, but does not mention any generative mechanism (e.g., autoregressive, decoder, diffusion). It is an encoder-only representation learning model, thus not generative.","The abstract describes CellPLM as a pre-trained transformer that treats cells as tokens and tissues as sentences, which is biological-token modeling without any natural-language text component; thus it fails the text_component criterion.","The abstract describes a pre-trained transformer for single-cell data (CellPLM) that encodes cell-cell relations, but does not mention any generative mechanism (e.g., autoregressive, decoder, diffusion). It is an encoder-only representation learning model, thus not generative.",,CellPLM: Pre-training of Cell Language Model Beyond Single Cells,"The current state-of-the-art single-cell pre-trained models are greatly inspired by the success of large language models. They trained transformers by treating genes as tokens and cells as sentences. However, three fundamental differences between single-cell data and natural language data are overlooked: (1) scRNA-seq data are presented as bag-of-genes instead of sequences of RNAs; (2) Cell-cell relations are more intricate and important than inter-sentence relations; and (3) The quantity of single-cell data is considerably inferior to text data, and they are very noisy. In light of these characteristics, we propose a new pre-trained model CellPLM , which takes cells as tokens and tissues as sentences. In addition, we leverage spatially-resolved transcriptomic data in pre-training to facilitate learning cell-cell relationships and introduce a Gaussian mixture prior distribution as an additional inductive bias to overcome data limitation. CellPLM is the first single-cell pre-trained transformer that encodes cell-cell relations and it consistently outperforms existing pre-trained and non-pre-trained models in diverse downstream tasks, with 100x times higher inference speed compared to existing pre-trained models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"The model uses cells as tokens and tissues as sentences on single-cell and spatial transcriptomic data, with no natural-language text component present. | The abstract describes a pre-trained transformer for single-cell data without any explicit generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion); thus it is not a generative model.","The model uses cells as tokens and tissues as sentences on single-cell and spatial transcriptomic data, with no natural-language text component present.","The abstract describes a pre-trained transformer for single-cell data without any explicit generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion); thus it is not a generative model.",,CellPLM: Pre-training of Cell Language Model Beyond Single Cells,"The current state-of-the-art single-cell pre-trained models are greatly inspired by the success of large language models. They trained transformers by treating genes as tokens and cells as sentences. However, three fundamental differences between single-cell data and natural language data are overlooked: (1) scRNA-seq data are presented as bag-of-genes instead of sequences of RNAs; (2) Cell-cell relations are more intricate and important than inter-sentence relations; and (3) The quantity of single-cell data is considerably inferior to text data, and they are very noisy. In light of these characteristics, we propose a new pre-trained model CellPLM , which takes cells as tokens and tissues as sentences. In addition, we leverage spatially-resolved transcriptomic data in pre-training to facilitate learning cell-cell relationships and introduce a Gaussian mixture prior distribution as an additional inductive bias to overcome data limitation. CellPLM is the first single-cell pre-trained transformer that encodes cell-cell relations and it consistently outperforms existing pre-trained and non-pre-trained models in diverse downstream tasks, with 100x times higher inference speed compared to existing pre-trained models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"The model treats cells and tissues as tokens and sentences, using biological data only; no natural-language text component is described. Therefore, it does not meet the text component criterion. | The abstract describes a pre-trained transformer for single-cell representation learning, with no mention of generative mechanisms such as decoder, autoregressive generation, or sequence generation. Hence it is not a generative model.","The model treats cells and tissues as tokens and sentences, using biological data only; no natural-language text component is described. Therefore, it does not meet the text component criterion.","The abstract describes a pre-trained transformer for single-cell representation learning, with no mention of generative mechanisms such as decoder, autoregressive generation, or sequence generation. Hence it is not a generative model.",,CellPLM: Pre-training of Cell Language Model Beyond Single Cells,"The current state-of-the-art single-cell pre-trained models are greatly inspired by the success of large language models. They trained transformers by treating genes as tokens and cells as sentences. However, three fundamental differences between single-cell data and natural language data are overlooked: (1) scRNA-seq data are presented as bag-of-genes instead of sequences of RNAs; (2) Cell-cell relations are more intricate and important than inter-sentence relations; and (3) The quantity of single-cell data is considerably inferior to text data, and they are very noisy. In light of these characteristics, we propose a new pre-trained model CellPLM , which takes cells as tokens and tissues as sentences. In addition, we leverage spatially-resolved transcriptomic data in pre-training to facilitate learning cell-cell relationships and introduce a Gaussian mixture prior distribution as an additional inductive bias to overcome data limitation. CellPLM is the first single-cell pre-trained transformer that encodes cell-cell relations and it consistently outperforms existing pre-trained and non-pre-trained models in diverse downstream tasks, with 100x times higher inference speed compared to existing pre-trained models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"The work proposes a new transformer for single‑cell and spatial transcriptomics data, but the abstract describes genes as tokens and cells/tissues as sentences, not natural‑language text, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents CellPLM as a pre‑trained transformer that encodes cells and tissues with broad downstream performance, but it does not mention any generative decoder, autoregressive or diffusion mechanism.","The work proposes a new transformer for single‑cell and spatial transcriptomics data, but the abstract describes genes as tokens and cells/tissues as sentences, not natural‑language text, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents CellPLM as a pre‑trained transformer that encodes cells and tissues with broad downstream performance, but it does not mention any generative decoder, autoregressive or diffusion mechanism.",,CellPLM: Pre-training of Cell Language Model Beyond Single Cells,"The current state-of-the-art single-cell pre-trained models are greatly inspired by the success of large language models. They trained transformers by treating genes as tokens and cells as sentences. However, three fundamental differences between single-cell data and natural language data are overlooked: (1) scRNA-seq data are presented as bag-of-genes instead of sequences of RNAs; (2) Cell-cell relations are more intricate and important than inter-sentence relations; and (3) The quantity of single-cell data is considerably inferior to text data, and they are very noisy. In light of these characteristics, we propose a new pre-trained model CellPLM , which takes cells as tokens and tissues as sentences. In addition, we leverage spatially-resolved transcriptomic data in pre-training to facilitate learning cell-cell relationships and introduce a Gaussian mixture prior distribution as an additional inductive bias to overcome data limitation. CellPLM is the first single-cell pre-trained transformer that encodes cell-cell relations and it consistently outperforms existing pre-trained and non-pre-trained models in diverse downstream tasks, with 100x times higher inference speed compared to existing pre-trained models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents CellPLM as a transformer pretrained on single‑cell and spatial transcriptomic data, treating genes as tokens and cells/tissues as sentences, but it does not mention natural‑language text, captions, or documents; thus there is no substantive text component nor a text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents CellPLM as a new pre‑trained transformer for single‑cell data and reports broad downstream performance, but it gives no indication of a decoder or generative mechanism, so it is not generative yet shows foundation‑model‑like pretraining and transferability.","The abstract presents CellPLM as a transformer pretrained on single‑cell and spatial transcriptomic data, treating genes as tokens and cells/tissues as sentences, but it does not mention natural‑language text, captions, or documents; thus there is no substantive text component nor a text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents CellPLM as a new pre‑trained transformer for single‑cell data and reports broad downstream performance, but it gives no indication of a decoder or generative mechanism, so it is not generative yet shows foundation‑model‑like pretraining and transferability.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:7:        Large-scale foundation models have recently opened a new avenue to artificial general intelligence for life sciences, showing great promise in the analysis of single-cell transcriptomic data. Nevertheless, such challenges as the tremendous number of signaling regions, extreme data sparsity, and the nearly binary nature of single-cell epigenomic data have prevented the construction of a foundation model for epigenomics thus far, though it is evident that abundant epigenomic properties such as chromatin accessibility provide more decisive insights into cell states than transcriptomics, shaping the chromatin regulatory landscapes that control transcription in distinct cell types. Here, we introduce EpiAgent, the first foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data, pretrained on a manually curated large-scale Human-scATAC-Corpus that is comprised of approximately 5 million cells and 35 billion tokens. EpiAgent encodes chromatin accessibility patterns of cells as concise “cell sentences,” and employs a bidirectional attention mechanism to capture cellular heterogeneity behind regulatory networks. With comprehensive benchmarks, we demonstrate that EpiAgent excels in typical downstream tasks, including unsupervised feature extraction, supervised cell type annotation, and data imputation. By incorporating external embeddings, EpiAgent facilitates the prediction of cellular responses to both out-of-sample stimulated and unseen genetic perturbations, as well as reference data integration and query data mapping. By simulating the knockout of key cis-regulatory elements, EpiAgent enables in-silico treatment for cancer analysis. We further extended zero-shot capabilities of EpiAgent, allowing direct cell type annotation on newly sequenced datasets without additional training.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"EpiAgent is a foundation model for single-cell epigenomics using cell sentences (biological tokens) and does not involve natural-language text or text-bio alignment. | EpiAgent uses bidirectional attention (encoder-only) for representation learning; tasks are predictive/classification/imputation, not generative. No decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or generation mechanism described.",EpiAgent is a foundation model for single-cell epigenomics using cell sentences (biological tokens) and does not involve natural-language text or text-bio alignment.,"EpiAgent uses bidirectional attention (encoder-only) for representation learning; tasks are predictive/classification/imputation, not generative. No decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or generation mechanism described.",,EpiAgent: foundation model for single-cell epigenomics,"Abstract
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:8:        Large-scale foundation models have recently opened a new avenue to artificial general intelligence for life sciences, showing great promise in the analysis of single-cell transcriptomic data. Nevertheless, such challenges as the tremendous number of signaling regions, extreme data sparsity, and the nearly binary nature of single-cell epigenomic data have prevented the construction of a foundation model for epigenomics thus far, though it is evident that abundant epigenomic properties such as chromatin accessibility provide more decisive insights into cell states than transcriptomics, shaping the chromatin regulatory landscapes that control transcription in distinct cell types. Here, we introduce EpiAgent, the first foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data, pretrained on a manually curated large-scale Human-scATAC-Corpus that is comprised of approximately 5 million cells and 35 billion tokens. EpiAgent encodes chromatin accessibility patterns of cells as concise “cell sentences,” and employs a bidirectional attention mechanism to capture cellular heterogeneity behind regulatory networks. With comprehensive benchmarks, we demonstrate that EpiAgent excels in typical downstream tasks, including unsupervised feature extraction, supervised cell type annotation, and data imputation. By incorporating external embeddings, EpiAgent facilitates the prediction of cellular responses to both out-of-sample stimulated and unseen genetic perturbations, as well as reference data integration and query data mapping. By simulating the knockout of key cis-regulatory elements, EpiAgent enables in-silico treatment for cancer analysis. We further extended zero-shot capabilities of EpiAgent, allowing direct cell type annotation on newly sequenced datasets without additional training.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"EpiAgent is a foundation model for single-cell epigenomics using 'cell sentences' which are biological tokens, not natural language text; no text component or text-bio bridge described. | The abstract describes an encoder-only bidirectional attention model for single-cell epigenomics; no generative mechanism (decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or generation of text/sequences) is mentioned.","EpiAgent is a foundation model for single-cell epigenomics using 'cell sentences' which are biological tokens, not natural language text; no text component or text-bio bridge described.","The abstract describes an encoder-only bidirectional attention model for single-cell epigenomics; no generative mechanism (decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or generation of text/sequences) is mentioned.",,EpiAgent: foundation model for single-cell epigenomics,"Abstract
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:9:        Large-scale foundation models have recently opened a new avenue to artificial general intelligence for life sciences, showing great promise in the analysis of single-cell transcriptomic data. Nevertheless, such challenges as the tremendous number of signaling regions, extreme data sparsity, and the nearly binary nature of single-cell epigenomic data have prevented the construction of a foundation model for epigenomics thus far, though it is evident that abundant epigenomic properties such as chromatin accessibility provide more decisive insights into cell states than transcriptomics, shaping the chromatin regulatory landscapes that control transcription in distinct cell types. Here, we introduce EpiAgent, the first foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data, pretrained on a manually curated large-scale Human-scATAC-Corpus that is comprised of approximately 5 million cells and 35 billion tokens. EpiAgent encodes chromatin accessibility patterns of cells as concise “cell sentences,” and employs a bidirectional attention mechanism to capture cellular heterogeneity behind regulatory networks. With comprehensive benchmarks, we demonstrate that EpiAgent excels in typical downstream tasks, including unsupervised feature extraction, supervised cell type annotation, and data imputation. By incorporating external embeddings, EpiAgent facilitates the prediction of cellular responses to both out-of-sample stimulated and unseen genetic perturbations, as well as reference data integration and query data mapping. By simulating the knockout of key cis-regulatory elements, EpiAgent enables in-silico treatment for cancer analysis. We further extended zero-shot capabilities of EpiAgent, allowing direct cell type annotation on newly sequenced datasets without additional training.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"EpiAgent models single-cell chromatin accessibility using 'cell sentences' (biological tokens), not natural-language text; thus no text component. | The abstract describes EpiAgent as a foundation model using bidirectional attention, not a generative architecture; no generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion) is mentioned. Therefore generative_model_present is no.","EpiAgent models single-cell chromatin accessibility using 'cell sentences' (biological tokens), not natural-language text; thus no text component.","The abstract describes EpiAgent as a foundation model using bidirectional attention, not a generative architecture; no generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion) is mentioned. Therefore generative_model_present is no.",,EpiAgent: foundation model for single-cell epigenomics,"Abstract
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:10:        Large-scale foundation models have recently opened a new avenue to artificial general intelligence for life sciences, showing great promise in the analysis of single-cell transcriptomic data. Nevertheless, such challenges as the tremendous number of signaling regions, extreme data sparsity, and the nearly binary nature of single-cell epigenomic data have prevented the construction of a foundation model for epigenomics thus far, though it is evident that abundant epigenomic properties such as chromatin accessibility provide more decisive insights into cell states than transcriptomics, shaping the chromatin regulatory landscapes that control transcription in distinct cell types. Here, we introduce EpiAgent, the first foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data, pretrained on a manually curated large-scale Human-scATAC-Corpus that is comprised of approximately 5 million cells and 35 billion tokens. EpiAgent encodes chromatin accessibility patterns of cells as concise “cell sentences,” and employs a bidirectional attention mechanism to capture cellular heterogeneity behind regulatory networks. With comprehensive benchmarks, we demonstrate that EpiAgent excels in typical downstream tasks, including unsupervised feature extraction, supervised cell type annotation, and data imputation. By incorporating external embeddings, EpiAgent facilitates the prediction of cellular responses to both out-of-sample stimulated and unseen genetic perturbations, as well as reference data integration and query data mapping. By simulating the knockout of key cis-regulatory elements, EpiAgent enables in-silico treatment for cancer analysis. We further extended zero-shot capabilities of EpiAgent, allowing direct cell type annotation on newly sequenced datasets without additional training.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents EpiAgent as a foundation model for single‑cell chromatin accessibility without mentioning natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident. | The abstract introduces EpiAgent as a new foundation model pretrained on 5 M cells (large‑scale pretraining) but only describes encoding with a bidirectional attention mechanism, with no mention of decoder or generative capability.","The abstract presents EpiAgent as a foundation model for single‑cell chromatin accessibility without mentioning natural‑language text, prompts, or documents, so no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge is evident.","The abstract introduces EpiAgent as a new foundation model pretrained on 5 M cells (large‑scale pretraining) but only describes encoding with a bidirectional attention mechanism, with no mention of decoder or generative capability.",,EpiAgent: foundation model for single-cell epigenomics,"Abstract
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:11:        Large-scale foundation models have recently opened a new avenue to artificial general intelligence for life sciences, showing great promise in the analysis of single-cell transcriptomic data. Nevertheless, such challenges as the tremendous number of signaling regions, extreme data sparsity, and the nearly binary nature of single-cell epigenomic data have prevented the construction of a foundation model for epigenomics thus far, though it is evident that abundant epigenomic properties such as chromatin accessibility provide more decisive insights into cell states than transcriptomics, shaping the chromatin regulatory landscapes that control transcription in distinct cell types. Here, we introduce EpiAgent, the first foundation model for single-cell chromatin accessibility data, pretrained on a manually curated large-scale Human-scATAC-Corpus that is comprised of approximately 5 million cells and 35 billion tokens. EpiAgent encodes chromatin accessibility patterns of cells as concise “cell sentences,” and employs a bidirectional attention mechanism to capture cellular heterogeneity behind regulatory networks. With comprehensive benchmarks, we demonstrate that EpiAgent excels in typical downstream tasks, including unsupervised feature extraction, supervised cell type annotation, and data imputation. By incorporating external embeddings, EpiAgent facilitates the prediction of cellular responses to both out-of-sample stimulated and unseen genetic perturbations, as well as reference data integration and query data mapping. By simulating the knockout of key cis-regulatory elements, EpiAgent enables in-silico treatment for cancer analysis. We further extended zero-shot capabilities of EpiAgent, allowing direct cell type annotation on newly sequenced datasets without additional training.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"The work presents a new foundation model for single‑cell chromatin accessibility (biological modality) but only tokenizes the data as “cell sentences” without natural‑language text, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents EpiAgent as a bidirectional encoder pretrained on a large scATAC corpus, with no mention of decoder or generative mechanisms, indicating no generative model; however it highlights extensive pretraining (5M cells, 35B tokens), satisfying foundation‑model evidence.","The work presents a new foundation model for single‑cell chromatin accessibility (biological modality) but only tokenizes the data as “cell sentences” without natural‑language text, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents EpiAgent as a bidirectional encoder pretrained on a large scATAC corpus, with no mention of decoder or generative mechanisms, indicating no generative model; however it highlights extensive pretraining (5M cells, 35B tokens), satisfying foundation‑model evidence.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:12:benchmark:5,GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,unclear,no,no,,,,,"GeneGPT augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI API tools to answer genomics questions, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model. | GeneGPT is a method that augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI APIs, not a new generative model. The paper does not propose a generative architecture or foundation model; it is an application wrapper.","GeneGPT augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI API tools to answer genomics questions, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model.","GeneGPT is a method that augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI APIs, not a new generative model. The paper does not propose a generative architecture or foundation model; it is an application wrapper.",,GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,no,,,,,"The paper augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI API tools for question answering, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model. | GeneGPT is a method that augments an existing generative LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs for genomics question answering; it does not introduce a new generative model or foundation model, making it an application wrapper.","The paper augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI API tools for question answering, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model.","GeneGPT is a method that augments an existing generative LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs for genomics question answering; it does not introduce a new generative model or foundation model, making it an application wrapper.",,GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,no,,,,,"GeneGPT augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs to answer genomics questions, which is an application wrapper around an external LLM, not a primary generative text-bio foundational model. | The paper describes GeneGPT as a method that augments an existing generative LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs for genomics question answering, which is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model.","GeneGPT augments an existing LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs to answer genomics questions, which is an application wrapper around an external LLM, not a primary generative text-bio foundational model.","The paper describes GeneGPT as a method that augments an existing generative LLM (Codex) with NCBI Web APIs for genomics question answering, which is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio foundational model.",,GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,yes,yes,answering genomics questions,none,augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract describes GeneGPT as a method that augments an existing LLM (Codex) with API calls to answer genomics questions, without introducing a new multimodal generative model. Hence it is a wrapper of a foundation model, and the text‑bio bridge is limited to tool use rather than a central multimodal model.","The abstract presents GeneGPT as a new method that augments an LLM with NCBI API calls to answer genomics questions, indicating a primary model with both a text component and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The paper augments an existing LLM (Codex) with a decoding algorithm to invoke NCBI APIs, constituting a wrapper around a pre‑trained model rather than introducing a new primary generative model.","The abstract describes GeneGPT as a method that augments an existing LLM (Codex) with API calls to answer genomics questions, without introducing a new multimodal generative model. Hence it is a wrapper of a foundation model, and the text‑bio bridge is limited to tool use rather than a central multimodal model.",GeneGPT: Augmenting Large Language Models with Domain Tools for Improved Access to Biomedical Information,"While large language models (LLMs) have been successfully applied to various tasks, they still face challenges with hallucinations. Augmenting LLMs with domain-specific tools such as database utilities can facilitate easier and more precise access to specialized knowledge. In this paper, we present GeneGPT, a novel method for teaching LLMs to use the Web APIs of the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) for answering genomics questions. Specifically, we prompt Codex to solve the GeneTuring tests with NCBI Web APIs by in-context learning and an augmented decoding algorithm that can detect and execute API calls. Experimental results show that GeneGPT achieves state-of-the-art performance on eight tasks in the GeneTuring benchmark with an average score of 0.83, largely surpassing retrieval-augmented LLMs such as the new Bing (0.44), biomedical LLMs such as BioMedLM (0.08) and BioGPT (0.04), as well as GPT-3 (0.16) and ChatGPT (0.12). Our further analyses suggest that: (1) API demonstrations have good cross-task generalizability and are more useful than documentations for in-context learning; (2) GeneGPT can generalize to longer chains of API calls and answer multi-hop questions in GeneHop, a novel dataset introduced in this work; (3) Different types of errors are enriched in different tasks, providing valuable insights for future improvements.",benchmark_boundary_case,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract describes GeneGPT, a novel method that augments an LLM with NCBI API calls to answer genomics questions, indicating a substantive natural‑language component and a direct text‑to‑biology bridge centered on genomic data. | The abstract presents GeneGPT with an augmented decoding algorithm that generates answers and triggers API calls, confirming a generative architecture, and leverages a large language model across eight biomedical tasks, indicating foundation-model-like breadth.","The abstract describes GeneGPT, a novel method that augments an LLM with NCBI API calls to answer genomics questions, indicating a substantive natural‑language component and a direct text‑to‑biology bridge centered on genomic data.","The abstract presents GeneGPT with an augmented decoding algorithm that generates answers and triggers API calls, confirming a generative architecture, and leverages a large language model across eight biomedical tasks, indicating foundation-model-like breadth.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:13:benchmark:4,GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,yes,uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5,none,none,wrapper_boundary,"GenePT uses frozen ChatGPT embeddings for downstream classification tasks, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model. No generative component is described.","GenePT uses frozen GPT-3.5 embeddings from NCBI text descriptions as features for downstream classification tasks, fitting the definition of an application wrapper rather than a primary text-bio model.","GenePT uses ChatGPT embeddings to produce gene and cell embeddings, but does not itself implement a generative architecture; it is an embedding-based method, not a generative model.","GenePT uses frozen ChatGPT embeddings for downstream classification tasks, making it an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model. No generative component is described.",GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,yes,no,yes,,,,,"GenePT uses external LLM (ChatGPT) embeddings from text descriptions as features for downstream biological tasks, without presenting a new multimodal generative model. This qualifies as an application wrapper around an existing LLM. | GenePT leverages precomputed GPT-3.5 embeddings to create cell representations for classification tasks; no new generative model is proposed, and the work is an application wrapper around an existing generative model.","GenePT uses external LLM (ChatGPT) embeddings from text descriptions as features for downstream biological tasks, without presenting a new multimodal generative model. This qualifies as an application wrapper around an existing LLM.","GenePT leverages precomputed GPT-3.5 embeddings to create cell representations for classification tasks; no new generative model is proposed, and the work is an application wrapper around an existing generative model.",,GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC3_not_generative,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,no,yes,,,,,"GenePT is a primary model that uses ChatGPT embeddings of gene text descriptions to build single-cell embeddings, satisfying both text component and text-bio bridge criteria. | GenePT uses precomputed ChatGPT embeddings for gene and cell representation; it does not describe a generative architecture for GenePT itself, and downstream tasks are classification, not generation.","GenePT is a primary model that uses ChatGPT embeddings of gene text descriptions to build single-cell embeddings, satisfying both text component and text-bio bridge criteria.","GenePT uses precomputed ChatGPT embeddings for gene and cell representation; it does not describe a generative architecture for GenePT itself, and downstream tasks are classification, not generation.",,GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,yes,NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings,uses ChatGPT embeddings of genes to generate single-cell embeddings by averaging weighted gene embeddings,generates single-cell embeddings by averaging gene embeddings weighted by expression level,wrapper_boundary,"GenePT leverages existing ChatGPT embeddings of gene literature to produce cell embeddings without training a new generative model, fitting the definition of an application wrapper; the text–bio interaction is limited to using text-derived embeddings as features, not a true multimodal generative bridge.","The paper proposes GenePT, which relies on ChatGPT‑derived gene embeddings to build cell representations; this is a wrapper around an external LLM rather than a new primary generative model.","The abstract introduces GenePT, a new model that builds gene and cell embeddings using ChatGPT-generated embeddings, with broad downstream evaluation. No decoder or explicit generative mechanism is described, so it is not a generative model.","GenePT leverages existing ChatGPT embeddings of gene literature to produce cell embeddings without training a new generative model, fitting the definition of an application wrapper; the text–bio interaction is limited to using text-derived embeddings as features, not a true multimodal generative bridge.",GenePT: A Simple But Effective Foundation Model for Genes and Cells Built From ChatGPT.,"There has been significant recent progress in leveraging large-scale gene expression data to develop foundation models for single-cell biology. Models such as Geneformer and scGPT implicitly learn gene and cellular functions from the gene expression profiles of millions of cells, which requires extensive data curation and resource-intensive training. Here we explore a much simpler alternative by leveraging ChatGPT embeddings of genes based on literature. Our proposal, GenePT, uses NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings. From there, GenePT generates single-cell embeddings in two ways: (i) by averaging the gene embeddings, weighted by each gene's expression level; or (ii) by creating a sentence embedding for each cell, using gene names ordered by the expression level. Without the need for dataset curation and additional pretraining, GenePT is efficient and easy to use. On many downstream tasks used to evaluate recent single-cell foundation models - e.g., classifying gene properties and cell types - GenePT achieves comparable, and often better, performance than Geneformer and other models. GenePT demonstrates that large language model embedding of literature is a simple and effective path for biological foundation models.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,yes,NCBI text descriptions of individual genes with GPT-3.5 to generate gene embeddings,none,none,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract reports using ChatGPT embeddings of gene literature to create gene and cell embeddings via averaging, without training a new generative model. This constitutes a wrapper around an existing LLM, so the paper is classified as an application wrapper and lacks a generative component.","The work introduces GenePT, a new method that leverages ChatGPT‑derived gene text embeddings to construct cell embeddings, qualifying as an application wrapper that uses an external LLM; it clearly involves gene expression data (biological modality) and natural‑language gene descriptions (text component) forming a text‑bio bridge.","GenePT introduces a new model that creates gene and cell embeddings using ChatGPT, but the abstract does not describe a decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism.","The abstract reports using ChatGPT embeddings of gene literature to create gene and cell embeddings via averaging, without training a new generative model. This constitutes a wrapper around an existing LLM, so the paper is classified as an application wrapper and lacks a generative component."
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:14:benchmark:9,Generative pretraining from large-scale transcriptomes: Implications for single-cell deciphering and clinical translation,"Exponential accumulation of single-cell transcriptomes poses great challenge for efficient assimilation. Here, we present an approach entitled tGPT towards integration of 22.3 million single-cell transcriptomes by modeling gene expression rankings as generative pretraining task. tGPT is conceptually simple in that it autoregressively models the ranking of a gene in the context of its preceding neighbors. We demonstrated the high performance of tGPT on a range of fundamental single-cell analysis tasks and novel applications on bulk tissues. The single-cell clusters and cell lineage trajectories derived from tGPT are highly aligned with known cell labels and states. The feature patterns of tumor bulk tissues learned by tGPT are associated with a wide range of genomic alteration events, prognosis and treatment outcome of immunotherapy. tGPT represents a new analytical paradigm for integrating and deciphering massive amount of transcriptome data and it will facilitate the interpretation and clinical translation of single-cell transcriptomes.",benchmark_boundary_case,Generative pretraining from large-scale transcriptomes: Implications for single-cell deciphering and clinical translation,"Exponential accumulation of single-cell transcriptomes poses great challenge for efficient assimilation. Here, we present an approach entitled tGPT towards integration of 22.3 million single-cell transcriptomes by modeling gene expression rankings as generative pretraining task. tGPT is conceptually simple in that it autoregressively models the ranking of a gene in the context of its preceding neighbors. We demonstrated the high performance of tGPT on a range of fundamental single-cell analysis tasks and novel applications on bulk tissues. The single-cell clusters and cell lineage trajectories derived from tGPT are highly aligned with known cell labels and states. The feature patterns of tumor bulk tissues learned by tGPT are associated with a wide range of genomic alteration events, prognosis and treatment outcome of immunotherapy. tGPT represents a new analytical paradigm for integrating and deciphering massive amount of transcriptome data and it will facilitate the interpretation and clinical translation of single-cell transcriptomes.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,yes,,,,,"tGPT models gene expression rankings autoregressively without any natural-language text, prompt, caption, or explicit text-bio alignment, so it does not meet the text component criterion. | The abstract presents tGPT, an autoregressive generative model pretrained on 22.3 million transcriptomes, demonstrating broad applicability across single-cell and bulk tissue tasks, which qualifies as a generative text-bio foundational model.","tGPT models gene expression rankings autoregressively without any natural-language text, prompt, caption, or explicit text-bio alignment, so it does not meet the text component criterion.","The abstract presents tGPT, an autoregressive generative model pretrained on 22.3 million transcriptomes, demonstrating broad applicability across single-cell and bulk tissue tasks, which qualifies as a generative text-bio foundational model.",,Generative pretraining from large-scale transcriptomes: Implications for single-cell deciphering and clinical translation,"Exponential accumulation of single-cell transcriptomes poses great challenge for efficient assimilation. Here, we present an approach entitled tGPT towards integration of 22.3 million single-cell transcriptomes by modeling gene expression rankings as generative pretraining task. tGPT is conceptually simple in that it autoregressively models the ranking of a gene in the context of its preceding neighbors. We demonstrated the high performance of tGPT on a range of fundamental single-cell analysis tasks and novel applications on bulk tissues. The single-cell clusters and cell lineage trajectories derived from tGPT are highly aligned with known cell labels and states. The feature patterns of tumor bulk tissues learned by tGPT are associated with a wide range of genomic alteration events, prognosis and treatment outcome of immunotherapy. tGPT represents a new analytical paradigm for integrating and deciphering massive amount of transcriptome data and it will facilitate the interpretation and clinical translation of single-cell transcriptomes.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,yes,,,,,"tGPT models gene expression rankings autoregressively; no natural language text or text-bio alignment present; excludes as text component missing. | The paper presents tGPT, an autoregressive model trained on gene expression rankings from 22.3 million single-cell transcriptomes with generative pretraining, but it operates on biological tokens (gene rankings) rather than natural language text.",tGPT models gene expression rankings autoregressively; no natural language text or text-bio alignment present; excludes as text component missing.,"The paper presents tGPT, an autoregressive model trained on gene expression rankings from 22.3 million single-cell transcriptomes with generative pretraining, but it operates on biological tokens (gene rankings) rather than natural language text.",,Generative pretraining from large-scale transcriptomes: Implications for single-cell deciphering and clinical translation,"Exponential accumulation of single-cell transcriptomes poses great challenge for efficient assimilation. Here, we present an approach entitled tGPT towards integration of 22.3 million single-cell transcriptomes by modeling gene expression rankings as generative pretraining task. tGPT is conceptually simple in that it autoregressively models the ranking of a gene in the context of its preceding neighbors. We demonstrated the high performance of tGPT on a range of fundamental single-cell analysis tasks and novel applications on bulk tissues. The single-cell clusters and cell lineage trajectories derived from tGPT are highly aligned with known cell labels and states. The feature patterns of tumor bulk tissues learned by tGPT are associated with a wide range of genomic alteration events, prognosis and treatment outcome of immunotherapy. tGPT represents a new analytical paradigm for integrating and deciphering massive amount of transcriptome data and it will facilitate the interpretation and clinical translation of single-cell transcriptomes.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents tGPT, which models gene expression rankings autoregressively, but there is no natural-language text component; it is biological token modeling only, thus fails the text component criterion. | Clearly generative (autoregressive) and pretrained on large transcriptome data (22.3M cells), but no natural language text component; thus text_component_boundary.","The abstract presents tGPT, which models gene expression rankings autoregressively, but there is no natural-language text component; it is biological token modeling only, thus fails the text component criterion.","Clearly generative (autoregressive) and pretrained on large transcriptome data (22.3M cells), but no natural language text component; thus text_component_boundary.",,Generative pretraining from large-scale transcriptomes: Implications for single-cell deciphering and clinical translation,"Exponential accumulation of single-cell transcriptomes poses great challenge for efficient assimilation. Here, we present an approach entitled tGPT towards integration of 22.3 million single-cell transcriptomes by modeling gene expression rankings as generative pretraining task. tGPT is conceptually simple in that it autoregressively models the ranking of a gene in the context of its preceding neighbors. We demonstrated the high performance of tGPT on a range of fundamental single-cell analysis tasks and novel applications on bulk tissues. The single-cell clusters and cell lineage trajectories derived from tGPT are highly aligned with known cell labels and states. The feature patterns of tumor bulk tissues learned by tGPT are associated with a wide range of genomic alteration events, prognosis and treatment outcome of immunotherapy. tGPT represents a new analytical paradigm for integrating and deciphering massive amount of transcriptome data and it will facilitate the interpretation and clinical translation of single-cell transcriptomes.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract describes tGPT as a generative pretraining model over gene expression rankings, with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, or captions, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents tGPT as an autoregressive generative model pretrained on 22.3 M single‑cell transcriptomes, showing broad pretraining and transfer to multiple downstream tasks.","The abstract describes tGPT as a generative pretraining model over gene expression rankings, with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, or captions, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents tGPT as an autoregressive generative model pretrained on 22.3 M single‑cell transcriptomes, showing broad pretraining and transfer to multiple downstream tasks.",,Generative pretraining from large-scale transcriptomes: Implications for single-cell deciphering and clinical translation,"Exponential accumulation of single-cell transcriptomes poses great challenge for efficient assimilation. Here, we present an approach entitled tGPT towards integration of 22.3 million single-cell transcriptomes by modeling gene expression rankings as generative pretraining task. tGPT is conceptually simple in that it autoregressively models the ranking of a gene in the context of its preceding neighbors. We demonstrated the high performance of tGPT on a range of fundamental single-cell analysis tasks and novel applications on bulk tissues. The single-cell clusters and cell lineage trajectories derived from tGPT are highly aligned with known cell labels and states. The feature patterns of tumor bulk tissues learned by tGPT are associated with a wide range of genomic alteration events, prognosis and treatment outcome of immunotherapy. tGPT represents a new analytical paradigm for integrating and deciphering massive amount of transcriptome data and it will facilitate the interpretation and clinical translation of single-cell transcriptomes.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract describes tGPT as a generative model that autoregressively ranks genes in transcriptomes, with no mention of natural-language text or textual inputs/outputs, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge. | The abstract describes tGPT as an autoregressive model that generatively pretrains on gene expression rankings and is pretrained on 22.3 M single‑cell transcriptomes, indicating a primary generative foundation model.","The abstract describes tGPT as a generative model that autoregressively ranks genes in transcriptomes, with no mention of natural-language text or textual inputs/outputs, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","The abstract describes tGPT as an autoregressive model that generatively pretrains on gene expression rankings and is pretrained on 22.3 M single‑cell transcriptomes, indicating a primary generative foundation model.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:16:benchmark:11,Nicheformer: a foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics,"Tissue makeup relies fundamentally on the cellular microenvironment. Spatial single-cell genomics allows probing the underlying cellular interactions in an unbiased, scalable fashion. To learn a unified cell representation that accounts for local dependencies in the cellular microenvironment, we propose Nicheformer, a transformer-based foundation model that combines human and mouse dissociated single-cell and targeted spatial transcriptomics data. Pretrained on over 57 million dissociated and 53 million spatially resolved cells across 73 tissues on cellular reconstruction, the model is fine-tuned on spatial tasks for spatial omics data to decode spatially resolved cellular information. Nicheformer excels in linear-probing and fine-tuning scenarios for a novel set of downstream tasks, in particular spatial composition prediction and spatial label prediction. We further show that existing foundation models trained on dissociated single-cell data alone are not capable of recapitulating the spatial complexity of cells in their microenvironments, indicating that multiscale models are required to understand complex local dependencies at scale. Nicheformer enables the prediction of the spatial context of dissociated cells, allowing the transfer of rich spatial information to scRNA-seq datasets. Overall, Nicheformer sets the stage for the next generation of machine-learning models in spatial single-cell analysis. Extended Abstract Tissue makeup and the corresponding orchestration of vital biological activities, ranging from development and differentiation to immune response and regeneration, rely fundamentally on the cellular microenvironment and the interactions between cells. Spatial single-cell genomics allows probing such interactions in an unbiased and, increasingly, scalable fashion. To learn a unified cell representation that accounts for local dependencies in the cellular microenvironment and the underlying cell interactions, we propose to generalize recent foundation modeling approaches for disassociated single-cell transcriptomics to the spatial omics setting. Our model, Nicheformer, is a transformer-based foundation model that combines human and mouse dissociated single-cell and targeted spatial transcriptomics data to learn a cellular representation useful for a large variety of downstream tasks. Nicheformer is pretrained on over 57 million dissociated and 53 million spatially resolved cells across 73 tissues from both human and mouse. Subsequently, the model is fine-tuned on spatial tasks for spatial omics data to decode spatially resolved cellular information. We demonstrate the usefulness of Nicheformer in both linear-probing as well as fine-tuning scenarios on a novel set of spatially-relevant downstream tasks such as spatial density prediction or niche and region label prediction. In particular, we show that Nicheformer enables the prediction of the spatial context of dissociated cells, allowing the transfer of rich spatial information to scRNA-seq datasets. We define a series of novel spatial prediction problems and observe consistent top performance of Nicheformer, demonstrating the advantage of the improved model capacity of the underlying transformer. Additionally, we benchmarked Nicheformer in these tasks against scGPT1, Geneformer2, scVI3 and PCA and show that the Nicheformer architecture excels in these tasks. Altogether, our large-scale resource of more than 110 million cells in a partial spatial context, together with the set of novel spatial learning tasks and the Nicheformer model itself, will pave the way for the next generation of machine-learning models for spatial single-cell analysis.",benchmark_boundary_case,Nicheformer: a foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics,"Tissue makeup relies fundamentally on the cellular microenvironment. Spatial single-cell genomics allows probing the underlying cellular interactions in an unbiased, scalable fashion. To learn a unified cell representation that accounts for local dependencies in the cellular microenvironment, we propose Nicheformer, a transformer-based foundation model that combines human and mouse dissociated single-cell and targeted spatial transcriptomics data. Pretrained on over 57 million dissociated and 53 million spatially resolved cells across 73 tissues on cellular reconstruction, the model is fine-tuned on spatial tasks for spatial omics data to decode spatially resolved cellular information. Nicheformer excels in linear-probing and fine-tuning scenarios for a novel set of downstream tasks, in particular spatial composition prediction and spatial label prediction. We further show that existing foundation models trained on dissociated single-cell data alone are not capable of recapitulating the spatial complexity of cells in their microenvironments, indicating that multiscale models are required to understand complex local dependencies at scale. Nicheformer enables the prediction of the spatial context of dissociated cells, allowing the transfer of rich spatial information to scRNA-seq datasets. Overall, Nicheformer sets the stage for the next generation of machine-learning models in spatial single-cell analysis. Extended Abstract Tissue makeup and the corresponding orchestration of vital biological activities, ranging from development and differentiation to immune response and regeneration, rely fundamentally on the cellular microenvironment and the interactions between cells. Spatial single-cell genomics allows probing such interactions in an unbiased and, increasingly, scalable fashion. To learn a unified cell representation that accounts for local dependencies in the cellular microenvironment and the underlying cell interactions, we propose to generalize recent foundation modeling approaches for disassociated single-cell transcriptomics to the spatial omics setting. Our model, Nicheformer, is a transformer-based foundation model that combines human and mouse dissociated single-cell and targeted spatial transcriptomics data to learn a cellular representation useful for a large variety of downstream tasks. Nicheformer is pretrained on over 57 million dissociated and 53 million spatially resolved cells across 73 tissues from both human and mouse. Subsequently, the model is fine-tuned on spatial tasks for spatial omics data to decode spatially resolved cellular information. We demonstrate the usefulness of Nicheformer in both linear-probing as well as fine-tuning scenarios on a novel set of spatially-relevant downstream tasks such as spatial density prediction or niche and region label prediction. In particular, we show that Nicheformer enables the prediction of the spatial context of dissociated cells, allowing the transfer of rich spatial information to scRNA-seq datasets. We define a series of novel spatial prediction problems and observe consistent top performance of Nicheformer, demonstrating the advantage of the improved model capacity of the underlying transformer. Additionally, we benchmarked Nicheformer in these tasks against scGPT1, Geneformer2, scVI3 and PCA and show that the Nicheformer architecture excels in these tasks. Altogether, our large-scale resource of more than 110 million cells in a partial spatial context, together with the set of novel spatial learning tasks and the Nicheformer model itself, will pave the way for the next generation of machine-learning models for spatial single-cell analysis.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics data with no mention of natural language text, text descriptions, prompts, or text-bio alignment, so it lacks a substantive text component. | Nicheformer is a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics, but it is used for predictive tasks (spatial composition prediction, label prediction) without any generative mechanism described; thus not a generative model.","The abstract presents a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics data with no mention of natural language text, text descriptions, prompts, or text-bio alignment, so it lacks a substantive text component.","Nicheformer is a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics, but it is used for predictive tasks (spatial composition prediction, label prediction) without any generative mechanism described; thus not a generative model.",,Nicheformer: a foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics,"Tissue makeup relies fundamentally on the cellular microenvironment. Spatial single-cell genomics allows probing the underlying cellular interactions in an unbiased, scalable fashion. To learn a unified cell representation that accounts for local dependencies in the cellular microenvironment, we propose Nicheformer, a transformer-based foundation model that combines human and mouse dissociated single-cell and targeted spatial transcriptomics data. Pretrained on over 57 million dissociated and 53 million spatially resolved cells across 73 tissues on cellular reconstruction, the model is fine-tuned on spatial tasks for spatial omics data to decode spatially resolved cellular information. Nicheformer excels in linear-probing and fine-tuning scenarios for a novel set of downstream tasks, in particular spatial composition prediction and spatial label prediction. We further show that existing foundation models trained on dissociated single-cell data alone are not capable of recapitulating the spatial complexity of cells in their microenvironments, indicating that multiscale models are required to understand complex local dependencies at scale. Nicheformer enables the prediction of the spatial context of dissociated cells, allowing the transfer of rich spatial information to scRNA-seq datasets. Overall, Nicheformer sets the stage for the next generation of machine-learning models in spatial single-cell analysis. Extended Abstract Tissue makeup and the corresponding orchestration of vital biological activities, ranging from development and differentiation to immune response and regeneration, rely fundamentally on the cellular microenvironment and the interactions between cells. Spatial single-cell genomics allows probing such interactions in an unbiased and, increasingly, scalable fashion. To learn a unified cell representation that accounts for local dependencies in the cellular microenvironment and the underlying cell interactions, we propose to generalize recent foundation modeling approaches for disassociated single-cell transcriptomics to the spatial omics setting. Our model, Nicheformer, is a transformer-based foundation model that combines human and mouse dissociated single-cell and targeted spatial transcriptomics data to learn a cellular representation useful for a large variety of downstream tasks. Nicheformer is pretrained on over 57 million dissociated and 53 million spatially resolved cells across 73 tissues from both human and mouse. Subsequently, the model is fine-tuned on spatial tasks for spatial omics data to decode spatially resolved cellular information. We demonstrate the usefulness of Nicheformer in both linear-probing as well as fine-tuning scenarios on a novel set of spatially-relevant downstream tasks such as spatial density prediction or niche and region label prediction. In particular, we show that Nicheformer enables the prediction of the spatial context of dissociated cells, allowing the transfer of rich spatial information to scRNA-seq datasets. We define a series of novel spatial prediction problems and observe consistent top performance of Nicheformer, demonstrating the advantage of the improved model capacity of the underlying transformer. Additionally, we benchmarked Nicheformer in these tasks against scGPT1, Geneformer2, scVI3 and PCA and show that the Nicheformer architecture excels in these tasks. Altogether, our large-scale resource of more than 110 million cells in a partial spatial context, together with the set of novel spatial learning tasks and the Nicheformer model itself, will pave the way for the next generation of machine-learning models for spatial single-cell analysis.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"The abstract describes a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics data with no mention of natural language text, prompts, or text conditioning; therefore it lacks a text component. | The abstract describes a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics that performs prediction tasks (spatial composition, label prediction) without explicit generative mechanism; no evidence of text generation or generative architecture.","The abstract describes a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics data with no mention of natural language text, prompts, or text conditioning; therefore it lacks a text component.","The abstract describes a transformer-based foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics that performs prediction tasks (spatial composition, label prediction) without explicit generative mechanism; no evidence of text generation or generative architecture.",,Nicheformer: a foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics,"Tissue makeup relies fundamentally on the cellular microenvironment. Spatial single-cell genomics allows probing the underlying cellular interactions in an unbiased, scalable fashion. To learn a unified cell representation that accounts for local dependencies in the cellular microenvironment, we propose Nicheformer, a transformer-based foundation model that combines human and mouse dissociated single-cell and targeted spatial transcriptomics data. Pretrained on over 57 million dissociated and 53 million spatially resolved cells across 73 tissues on cellular reconstruction, the model is fine-tuned on spatial tasks for spatial omics data to decode spatially resolved cellular information. Nicheformer excels in linear-probing and fine-tuning scenarios for a novel set of downstream tasks, in particular spatial composition prediction and spatial label prediction. We further show that existing foundation models trained on dissociated single-cell data alone are not capable of recapitulating the spatial complexity of cells in their microenvironments, indicating that multiscale models are required to understand complex local dependencies at scale. Nicheformer enables the prediction of the spatial context of dissociated cells, allowing the transfer of rich spatial information to scRNA-seq datasets. Overall, Nicheformer sets the stage for the next generation of machine-learning models in spatial single-cell analysis. Extended Abstract Tissue makeup and the corresponding orchestration of vital biological activities, ranging from development and differentiation to immune response and regeneration, rely fundamentally on the cellular microenvironment and the interactions between cells. Spatial single-cell genomics allows probing such interactions in an unbiased and, increasingly, scalable fashion. To learn a unified cell representation that accounts for local dependencies in the cellular microenvironment and the underlying cell interactions, we propose to generalize recent foundation modeling approaches for disassociated single-cell transcriptomics to the spatial omics setting. Our model, Nicheformer, is a transformer-based foundation model that combines human and mouse dissociated single-cell and targeted spatial transcriptomics data to learn a cellular representation useful for a large variety of downstream tasks. Nicheformer is pretrained on over 57 million dissociated and 53 million spatially resolved cells across 73 tissues from both human and mouse. Subsequently, the model is fine-tuned on spatial tasks for spatial omics data to decode spatially resolved cellular information. We demonstrate the usefulness of Nicheformer in both linear-probing as well as fine-tuning scenarios on a novel set of spatially-relevant downstream tasks such as spatial density prediction or niche and region label prediction. In particular, we show that Nicheformer enables the prediction of the spatial context of dissociated cells, allowing the transfer of rich spatial information to scRNA-seq datasets. We define a series of novel spatial prediction problems and observe consistent top performance of Nicheformer, demonstrating the advantage of the improved model capacity of the underlying transformer. Additionally, we benchmarked Nicheformer in these tasks against scGPT1, Geneformer2, scVI3 and PCA and show that the Nicheformer architecture excels in these tasks. Altogether, our large-scale resource of more than 110 million cells in a partial spatial context, together with the set of novel spatial learning tasks and the Nicheformer model itself, will pave the way for the next generation of machine-learning models for spatial single-cell analysis.",benchmark_boundary_case,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"Nicheformer is a transformer foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics, but no natural-language text component is described; it uses biological tokens only. | The abstract describes a transformer-based foundation model pretrained on single-cell and spatial omics data for representation learning and downstream prediction tasks (spatial composition, label prediction), with no explicit generative mechanism or text component.","Nicheformer is a transformer foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics, but no natural-language text component is described; it uses biological tokens only.","The abstract describes a transformer-based foundation model pretrained on single-cell and spatial omics data for representation learning and downstream prediction tasks (spatial composition, label prediction), with no explicit generative mechanism or text component.",,Nicheformer: a foundation model for single-cell and spatial omics,"Tissue makeup relies fundamentally on the cellular microenvironment. Spatial single-cell genomics allows probing the underlying cellular interactions in an unbiased, scalable fashion. To learn a unified cell representation that accounts for local dependencies in the cellular microenvironment, we propose Nicheformer, a transformer-based foundation model that combines human and mouse dissociated single-cell and targeted spatial transcriptomics data. Pretrained on over 57 million dissociated and 53 million spatially resolved cells across 73 tissues on cellular reconstruction, the model is fine-tuned on spatial tasks for spatial omics data to decode spatially resolved cellular information. Nicheformer excels in linear-probing and fine-tuning scenarios for a novel set of downstream tasks, in particular spatial composition prediction and spatial label prediction. We further show that existing foundation models trained on dissociated single-cell data alone are not capable of recapitulating the spatial complexity of cells in their microenvironments, indicating that multiscale models are required to understand complex local dependencies at scale. Nicheformer enables the prediction of the spatial context of dissociated cells, allowing the transfer of rich spatial information to scRNA-seq datasets. Overall, Nicheformer sets the stage for the next generation of machine-learning models in spatial single-cell analysis. Extended Abstract Tissue makeup and the corresponding orchestration of vital biological activities, ranging from development and differentiation to immune response and regeneration, rely fundamentally on the cellular microenvironment and the interactions between cells. Spatial single-cell genomics allows probing such intera