./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:64:927,Computational Design of Synthetic Optical Barcodes in Microdroplets,"Barcodes are useful for identifying objects across time, space, and information modalities. However, materializing and decoding optical and multimodal barcodes on microscopic objects remains difficult despite the increasing need for multiplexed cell analysis. Here, a computational design of randomly combinatorial is presented, yet decodable barcodes in microdroplets. The design is based on a novel Real2Sim2Real framework: it first collects experimental images of optically distinct microparticles, then simulates massive combinatorial images by randomly assembling the imaged particles to train a neural network‐based decoder. It is demonstrated that the decoder, even though trained via simulation, accurately identifies the randomly assembled particles in real hydrogel microdroplets. It also shows that the microdroplets with an additional DNA barcoding functionality are applicable to individually link independently measured microscopic images and transcriptome profiles of pooled single cells.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Computational Design of Synthetic Optical Barcodes in Microdroplets,"Barcodes are useful for identifying objects across time, space, and information modalities. However, materializing and decoding optical and multimodal barcodes on microscopic objects remains difficult despite the increasing need for multiplexed cell analysis. Here, a computational design of randomly combinatorial is presented, yet decodable barcodes in microdroplets. The design is based on a novel Real2Sim2Real framework: it first collects experimental images of optically distinct microparticles, then simulates massive combinatorial images by randomly assembling the imaged particles to train a neural network‐based decoder. It is demonstrated that the decoder, even though trained via simulation, accurately identifies the randomly assembled particles in real hydrogel microdroplets. It also shows that the microdroplets with an additional DNA barcoding functionality are applicable to individually link independently measured microscopic images and transcriptome profiles of pooled single cells.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The abstract describes a barcode decoding framework using microscopy images and transcriptome profiles, with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, captions, or any text-bio alignment, so the text component criterion is not met. | The paper describes a neural network decoder for identifying optical barcodes, not a generative architecture; no generative mechanism such as autoregressive, diffusion, or sequence generation is mentioned.","The abstract describes a barcode decoding framework using microscopy images and transcriptome profiles, with no mention of natural-language text, prompts, captions, or any text-bio alignment, so the text component criterion is not met.","The paper describes a neural network decoder for identifying optical barcodes, not a generative architecture; no generative mechanism such as autoregressive, diffusion, or sequence generation is mentioned.",,Computational Design of Synthetic Optical Barcodes in Microdroplets,"Barcodes are useful for identifying objects across time, space, and information modalities. However, materializing and decoding optical and multimodal barcodes on microscopic objects remains difficult despite the increasing need for multiplexed cell analysis. Here, a computational design of randomly combinatorial is presented, yet decodable barcodes in microdroplets. The design is based on a novel Real2Sim2Real framework: it first collects experimental images of optically distinct microparticles, then simulates massive combinatorial images by randomly assembling the imaged particles to train a neural network‐based decoder. It is demonstrated that the decoder, even though trained via simulation, accurately identifies the randomly assembled particles in real hydrogel microdroplets. It also shows that the microdroplets with an additional DNA barcoding functionality are applicable to individually link independently measured microscopic images and transcriptome profiles of pooled single cells.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"No text/language component; paper focuses on optical barcode design and decoding, not generative text-bio modeling. | The abstract describes a neural network decoder for identifying barcodes in microdroplets, not a generative model. No generative mechanism (e.g., autoregressive, diffusion, VAE) is mentioned.","No text/language component; paper focuses on optical barcode design and decoding, not generative text-bio modeling.","The abstract describes a neural network decoder for identifying barcodes in microdroplets, not a generative model. No generative mechanism (e.g., autoregressive, diffusion, VAE) is mentioned.",,Computational Design of Synthetic Optical Barcodes in Microdroplets,"Barcodes are useful for identifying objects across time, space, and information modalities. However, materializing and decoding optical and multimodal barcodes on microscopic objects remains difficult despite the increasing need for multiplexed cell analysis. Here, a computational design of randomly combinatorial is presented, yet decodable barcodes in microdroplets. The design is based on a novel Real2Sim2Real framework: it first collects experimental images of optically distinct microparticles, then simulates massive combinatorial images by randomly assembling the imaged particles to train a neural network‐based decoder. It is demonstrated that the decoder, even though trained via simulation, accurately identifies the randomly assembled particles in real hydrogel microdroplets. It also shows that the microdroplets with an additional DNA barcoding functionality are applicable to individually link independently measured microscopic images and transcriptome profiles of pooled single cells.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,No text/language component; the paper describes a barcode decoding framework with no natural-language text or text conditioning. | The abstract describes a neural network decoder for identifying optical barcodes in microdroplets; no generative model or text-bio foundation model is described.,No text/language component; the paper describes a barcode decoding framework with no natural-language text or text conditioning.,The abstract describes a neural network decoder for identifying optical barcodes in microdroplets; no generative model or text-bio foundation model is described.,,Computational Design of Synthetic Optical Barcodes in Microdroplets,"Barcodes are useful for identifying objects across time, space, and information modalities. However, materializing and decoding optical and multimodal barcodes on microscopic objects remains difficult despite the increasing need for multiplexed cell analysis. Here, a computational design of randomly combinatorial is presented, yet decodable barcodes in microdroplets. The design is based on a novel Real2Sim2Real framework: it first collects experimental images of optically distinct microparticles, then simulates massive combinatorial images by randomly assembling the imaged particles to train a neural network‐based decoder. It is demonstrated that the decoder, even though trained via simulation, accurately identifies the randomly assembled particles in real hydrogel microdroplets. It also shows that the microdroplets with an additional DNA barcoding functionality are applicable to individually link independently measured microscopic images and transcriptome profiles of pooled single cells.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The abstract presents a new Real2Sim2Real framework with a neural network decoder for optical barcodes and mentions linking microdroplet images to transcriptome profiles, indicating a biological modality. No natural-language text, prompts, or captions are described, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents a neural‑network decoder for synthetic optical barcodes and a simulation pipeline, but it does not describe a generative architecture (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion). No evidence of broad pretraining or foundation‑model characteristics is provided.","The abstract presents a new Real2Sim2Real framework with a neural network decoder for optical barcodes and mentions linking microdroplet images to transcriptome profiles, indicating a biological modality. No natural-language text, prompts, or captions are described, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents a neural‑network decoder for synthetic optical barcodes and a simulation pipeline, but it does not describe a generative architecture (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion). No evidence of broad pretraining or foundation‑model characteristics is provided.",,Computational Design of Synthetic Optical Barcodes in Microdroplets,"Barcodes are useful for identifying objects across time, space, and information modalities. However, materializing and decoding optical and multimodal barcodes on microscopic objects remains difficult despite the increasing need for multiplexed cell analysis. Here, a computational design of randomly combinatorial is presented, yet decodable barcodes in microdroplets. The design is based on a novel Real2Sim2Real framework: it first collects experimental images of optically distinct microparticles, then simulates massive combinatorial images by randomly assembling the imaged particles to train a neural network‐based decoder. It is demonstrated that the decoder, even though trained via simulation, accurately identifies the randomly assembled particles in real hydrogel microdroplets. It also shows that the microdroplets with an additional DNA barcoding functionality are applicable to individually link independently measured microscopic images and transcriptome profiles of pooled single cells.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The abstract introduces a new Real2Sim2Real framework and neural decoder for optical barcodes linked to transcriptome profiles, but it does not mention any natural-language text, prompts, or text‑bio mapping, so it lacks a substantive text component and bridge. | The abstract presents a decoder for optical barcodes and does not mention any generative mechanism such as decoder‑based generation, autoregressive or diffusion; therefore it is not a generative text‑bio foundational model.","The abstract introduces a new Real2Sim2Real framework and neural decoder for optical barcodes linked to transcriptome profiles, but it does not mention any natural-language text, prompts, or text‑bio mapping, so it lacks a substantive text component and bridge.","The abstract presents a decoder for optical barcodes and does not mention any generative mechanism such as decoder‑based generation, autoregressive or diffusion; therefore it is not a generative text‑bio foundational model.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:65:780,DNA sequence classification for diabetes mellitus using NuSVC and XGBoost: A comparative,"Diabetes Mellitus is a global health concern, characterized by high blood sugar levels over a prolonged period, leading to severe complications if left unmanaged. The early identification of individuals at risk is critical for effective intervention and treatment. Traditional diagnostic methods rely heavily on clinical symptoms and biochemical tests, which may not capture the underlying genetic predispositions. With the advent of genomics, DNA sequence analysis has emerged as a promising approach to uncover the genetic markers associated with Diabetes Mellitus. However, the challenge lies in accurately classifying DNA sequences to predict susceptibility to the disease, given the complex nature of genetic data. This study addresses this challenge by employing two advanced machine learning models, NuSVC (Nu-Support Vector Classification) and XGBoost (Extreme Gradient Boosting), to classify DNA sequences related to Diabetes Mellitus. The dataset, obtained from reputable sources like NCBI, was preprocessed using Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques, where DNA sequences were treated as textual data and transformed into numerical features using TF-IDF (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency). To handle the class imbalance in the dataset, SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) was applied. The models were trained and validated using 10-fold cross-validation. XGBoost was trained with up to 300 boosting rounds, and performance was evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, ROC-AUC, and log loss. The results demonstrate that XGBoost outperformed NuSVC across all metrics, achieving an accuracy of 98%, a log loss of 0.0650, and an AUC of 1.00, compared to NuSVC’s accuracy of 87%, log loss of 0.2649, and AUC of 0.95. The superior performance of XGBoost indicates its robustness in handling complex genetic data and its potential utility in clinical applications for early diagnosis of Diabetes Mellitus. The findings of this study underscore the importance of advanced machine learning techniques in genomics and suggest that integrating such models into healthcare systems could significantly enhance predictive diagnostics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,DNA sequence classification for diabetes mellitus using NuSVC and XGBoost: A comparative,"Diabetes Mellitus is a global health concern, characterized by high blood sugar levels over a prolonged period, leading to severe complications if left unmanaged. The early identification of individuals at risk is critical for effective intervention and treatment. Traditional diagnostic methods rely heavily on clinical symptoms and biochemical tests, which may not capture the underlying genetic predispositions. With the advent of genomics, DNA sequence analysis has emerged as a promising approach to uncover the genetic markers associated with Diabetes Mellitus. However, the challenge lies in accurately classifying DNA sequences to predict susceptibility to the disease, given the complex nature of genetic data. This study addresses this challenge by employing two advanced machine learning models, NuSVC (Nu-Support Vector Classification) and XGBoost (Extreme Gradient Boosting), to classify DNA sequences related to Diabetes Mellitus. The dataset, obtained from reputable sources like NCBI, was preprocessed using Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques, where DNA sequences were treated as textual data and transformed into numerical features using TF-IDF (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency). To handle the class imbalance in the dataset, SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) was applied. The models were trained and validated using 10-fold cross-validation. XGBoost was trained with up to 300 boosting rounds, and performance was evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, ROC-AUC, and log loss. The results demonstrate that XGBoost outperformed NuSVC across all metrics, achieving an accuracy of 98%, a log loss of 0.0650, and an AUC of 1.00, compared to NuSVC’s accuracy of 87%, log loss of 0.2649, and AUC of 0.95. The superior performance of XGBoost indicates its robustness in handling complex genetic data and its potential utility in clinical applications for early diagnosis of Diabetes Mellitus. The findings of this study underscore the importance of advanced machine learning techniques in genomics and suggest that integrating such models into healthcare systems could significantly enhance predictive diagnostics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The paper classifies DNA sequences using NuSVC and XGBoost with TF-IDF features; it does not describe a generative model, lacks a natural-language text component, and has no text-bio bridge. | The paper uses NuSVC and XGBoost for DNA sequence classification, which are predictive classifiers, not generative models. No mention of generative architecture or foundation model characteristics.","The paper classifies DNA sequences using NuSVC and XGBoost with TF-IDF features; it does not describe a generative model, lacks a natural-language text component, and has no text-bio bridge.","The paper uses NuSVC and XGBoost for DNA sequence classification, which are predictive classifiers, not generative models. No mention of generative architecture or foundation model characteristics.",,DNA sequence classification for diabetes mellitus using NuSVC and XGBoost: A comparative,"Diabetes Mellitus is a global health concern, characterized by high blood sugar levels over a prolonged period, leading to severe complications if left unmanaged. The early identification of individuals at risk is critical for effective intervention and treatment. Traditional diagnostic methods rely heavily on clinical symptoms and biochemical tests, which may not capt