emented to ensure training stability and enhance computational efficiency, which collectively reduces costs and facilitates million-basepair context modeling. Functionally, Genos performs single-nucleotide resolution analysis and dynamically simulates the cascade effects of noncoding variations on RNA expression profiles. In comprehensive evaluations, Genos uniformly surpasses state-of-the-art models on critical human genomics benchmarks and demonstrates robust omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities. We present a systematic technical evaluation and validation of Genos’s architecture, training convergence, and performance across standard benchmarks. Conclusions This work provides a reliable technical blueprint and performance benchmark for the development of the next generation of high-efficiency genomic foundation models. Genos model weights, inference code, and usage documentation are publicly available on GitHub (https://github.com/BGI-HangzhouAI/Genos) and Hugging Face Hub (https://huggingface.co/BGI-HangzhouAI).",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,primary_model_paper,unclear,unclear,unclear,yes,omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,dynamically simulates the cascade effects of noncoding variations,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract introduces Genos as a genomic foundation model but only ambiguously references 'omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities' without clear natural-language text or text-bio alignment. No explicit generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive) is described. These unresolved criteria make the record a borderline case due to insufficient abstract detail.","The abstract presents Genos as a genomic foundation model for sequence modeling, with a brief mention of 'omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities' but no clear description of a text component or text-bio bridge as part of the model itself.","The abstract presents a new genomic foundation model with million-basepair context modeling and simulation of variation effects, but does not explicitly describe a generative mechanism (e.g., autoregressive, diffusion) for sequence generation, leaving generative status unclear.","The abstract introduces Genos as a genomic foundation model but only ambiguously references 'omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities' without clear natural-language text or text-bio alignment. No explicit generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive) is described. These unresolved criteria make the record a borderline case due to insufficient abstract detail.",Genos: a human-centric genomic foundation model.,"Abstract Background The rapid expansion of human genomic data demands foundation models that manage ultra-long sequences and capture population diversity, limitations common in existing models that lack human-specific representation, and clinical inference efficiency. Results Here, we introduce Genos (Genos-1.2B/Genos-10B), a human-centric genomic foundation model engineered for million-basepair sequence modeling. Genos utilizes a large-scale mixture of experts structure, optimized for a 1-Mb context, trained on high-quality human de novo assemblies from datasets such as the Human Pangenome Reference Consortium and the Human Genome Structural Variation Consortium, representing diverse global populations. A suite of optimization strategies was implemented to ensure training stability and enhance computational efficiency, which collectively reduces costs and facilitates million-basepair context modeling. Functionally, Genos performs single-nucleotide resolution analysis and dynamically simulates the cascade effects of noncoding variations on RNA expression profiles. In comprehensive evaluations, Genos uniformly surpasses state-of-the-art models on critical human genomics benchmarks and demonstrates robust omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities. We present a systematic technical evaluation and validation of Genos’s architecture, training convergence, and performance across standard benchmarks. Conclusions This work provides a reliable technical blueprint and performance benchmark for the development of the next generation of high-efficiency genomic foundation models. Genos model weights, inference code, and usage documentation are publicly available on GitHub (https://github.com/BGI-HangzhouAI/Genos) and Hugging Face Hub (https://huggingface.co/BGI-HangzhouAI).",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC3_not_generative,text_component_unclear,primary_model_paper,unclear,unclear,no,yes,omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,none,clear_exclude,"Genos is a genomic foundation model for sequence modeling; no explicit generative mechanism described (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion). Text component and text-bio bridge are unclear from abstract; only ambiguous 'omics-text cross-modal' phrase. Therefore excluded as not generative (EC3).",The abstract describes a genomic foundation model for sequence modeling with no clear natural-language text component; only a vague mention of 'omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities' without detail.,"The abstract describes a genomic foundation model (Genos) for sequence modeling and simulation of noncoding variant effects on expression, but no generative mechanism (e.g., autoregressive, decoder, text generation) is mentioned.","Genos is a genomic foundation model for sequence modeling; no explicit generative mechanism described (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion). Text component and text-bio bridge are unclear from abstract; only ambiguous 'omics-text cross-modal' phrase. Therefore excluded as not generative (EC3).",Genos: a human-centric genomic foundation model.,"Abstract Background The rapid expansion of human genomic data demands foundation models that manage ultra-long sequences and capture population diversity, limitations common in existing models that lack human-specific representation, and clinical inference efficiency. Results Here, we introduce Genos (Genos-1.2B/Genos-10B), a human-centric genomic foundation model engineered for million-basepair sequence modeling. Genos utilizes a large-scale mixture of experts structure, optimized for a 1-Mb context, trained on high-quality human de novo assemblies from datasets such as the Human Pangenome Reference Consortium and the Human Genome Structural Variation Consortium, representing diverse global populations. A suite of optimization strategies was implemented to ensure training stability and enhance computational efficiency, which collectively reduces costs and facilitates million-basepair context modeling. Functionally, Genos performs single-nucleotide resolution analysis and dynamically simulates the cascade effects of noncoding variations on RNA expression profiles. In comprehensive evaluations, Genos uniformly surpasses state-of-the-art models on critical human genomics benchmarks and demonstrates robust omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities. We present a systematic technical evaluation and validation of Genos’s architecture, training convergence, and performance across standard benchmarks. Conclusions This work provides a reliable technical blueprint and performance benchmark for the development of the next generation of high-efficiency genomic foundation models. Genos model weights, inference code, and usage documentation are publicly available on GitHub (https://github.com/BGI-HangzhouAI/Genos) and Hugging Face Hub (https://huggingface.co/BGI-HangzhouAI).",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,,,,,"The abstract describes a genomic foundation model (Genos) for ultra‑long DNA sequences with no mention of natural‑language text input, output, or conditioning, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge. | The abstract introduces Genos as a genomic foundation model with large-scale pretraining, but it does not explicitly describe a generative architecture such as a decoder or autoregressive mechanism.","The abstract describes a genomic foundation model (Genos) for ultra‑long DNA sequences with no mention of natural‑language text input, output, or conditioning, so there is no substantive text component or text‑bio bridge.","The abstract introduces Genos as a genomic foundation model with large-scale pretraining, but it does not explicitly describe a generative architecture such as a decoder or autoregressive mechanism.",,Genos: a human-centric genomic foundation model.,"Abstract Background The rapid expansion of human genomic data demands foundation models that manage ultra-long sequences and capture population diversity, limitations common in existing models that lack human-specific representation, and clinical inference efficiency. Results Here, we introduce Genos (Genos-1.2B/Genos-10B), a human-centric genomic foundation model engineered for million-basepair sequence modeling. Genos utilizes a large-scale mixture of experts structure, optimized for a 1-Mb context, trained on high-quality human de novo assemblies from datasets such as the Human Pangenome Reference Consortium and the Human Genome Structural Variation Consortium, representing diverse global populations. A suite of optimization strategies was implemented to ensure training stability and enhance computational efficiency, which collectively reduces costs and facilitates million-basepair context modeling. Functionally, Genos performs single-nucleotide resolution analysis and dynamically simulates the cascade effects of noncoding variations on RNA expression profiles. In comprehensive evaluations, Genos uniformly surpasses state-of-the-art models on critical human genomics benchmarks and demonstrates robust omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities. We present a systematic technical evaluation and validation of Genos’s architecture, training convergence, and performance across standard benchmarks. Conclusions This work provides a reliable technical blueprint and performance benchmark for the development of the next generation of high-efficiency genomic foundation models. Genos model weights, inference code, and usage documentation are publicly available on GitHub (https://github.com/BGI-HangzhouAI/Genos) and Hugging Face Hub (https://huggingface.co/BGI-HangzhouAI).",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,primary_model_paper,unclear,unclear,no,yes,omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,none,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract clearly presents a new genomic foundation model (primary_model_paper) and includes a biological modality (genomic sequences). It mentions ""omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,"" but does not explicitly describe natural-language text inputs or outputs, leaving the presence of a text component and a text‑bio bridge ambiguous. No generative mechanism (decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, etc.) is reported, so the model is non‑generative.","The paper introduces a new genomic foundation model (Genos) and clearly involves a biological modality (human genomic sequences). The abstract mentions ""omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,"" suggesting a text component, but it does not specify natural-language text or a concrete text‑bio mapping, leaving the presence of a substantive text component and bridge ambiguous.","The abstract presents Genos as a genomic foundation model with large‑scale pretraining but does not describe a decoder, autoregressive or other generative mechanism, so no generative model is evident; foundation‑model characteristics are clearly stated.","The abstract clearly presents a new genomic foundation model (primary_model_paper) and includes a biological modality (genomic sequences). It mentions ""omics-text cross-modal diagnostic capabilities,"" but does not explicitly describe natural-language text inputs or outputs, leaving the presence of a text component and a text‑bio bridge ambiguous. No generative mechanism (decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, etc.) is reported, so the model is non‑generative."
