pecific molecular modules using network-based approach and cell line drug-gene signature data. We further estimated their treatment effects using two large-scale real-world patient databases; the real-world evidence we gained highlighted the potential of metformin in ameliorating PD progression. In conclusion, this work helps better understand clinical and pathophysiological complexity of PD progression and accelerate precision medicine.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The study uses machine learning on multimodal biological data (clinical, CSF, imaging, genetics) without presenting a generative text‑bio model or any natural‑language component. | The abstract describes PD subtyping and drug repurposing using multimodal data and existing ML/DL methods, with no mention of a generative architecture.","The study uses machine learning on multimodal biological data (clinical, CSF, imaging, genetics) without presenting a generative text‑bio model or any natural‑language component.","The abstract describes PD subtyping and drug repurposing using multimodal data and existing ML/DL methods, with no mention of a generative architecture.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:147:177,Integrating Imaging-Derived Clinical Endotypes with Plasma Proteomics and External Polygenic Risk Scores Enhances Coronary Microvascular Disease Risk Prediction.,"Coronary microvascular disease (CMVD) is an underdiagnosed but significant contributor to the burden of ischemic heart disease, characterized by angina and myocardial infarction. The development of risk prediction models such as polygenic risk scores (PRS) for CMVD has been limited by a lack of large-scale genome-wide association studies (GWAS). However, there is significant overlap between CMVD and enrollment criteria for coronary artery disease (CAD) GWAS. In this study, we developed CMVD PRS models by selecting variants identified in a CMVD GWAS and applying weights from an external CAD GWAS, using CMVD-associated loci as proxies for the genetic risk. We integrated plasma proteomics, clinical measures from perfusion PET imaging, and PRS to evaluate their contributions to CMVD risk prediction in comprehensive machine and deep learning models. We then developed a novel unsupervised endotyping framework for CMVD from perfusion PET-derived myocardial blood flow data, revealing distinct patient subgroups beyond traditional case-control definitions. This imaging-based stratification substantially improved classification performance alongside plasma proteomics and PRS, achieving AUROCs between 0.65 and 0.73 per class, significantly outperforming binary classifiers and existing clinical models, highlighting the potential of this stratification approach to enable more precise and personalized diagnosis by capturing the underlying heterogeneity of CMVD. This work represents the first application of imaging-based endotyping and the integration of genetic and proteomic data for CMVD risk prediction, establishing a framework for multimodal modeling in complex diseases.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Integrating Imaging-Derived Clinical Endotypes with Plasma Proteomics and External Polygenic Risk Scores Enhances Coronary Microvascular Disease Risk Prediction.,"Coronary microvascular disease (CMVD) is an underdiagnosed but significant contributor to the burden of ischemic heart disease, characterized by angina and myocardial infarction. The development of risk prediction models such as polygenic risk scores (PRS) for CMVD has been limited by a lack of large-scale genome-wide association studies (GWAS). However, there is significant overlap between CMVD and enrollment criteria for coronary artery disease (CAD) GWAS. In this study, we developed CMVD PRS models by selecting variants identified in a CMVD GWAS and applying weights from an external CAD GWAS, using CMVD-associated loci as proxies for the genetic risk. We integrated plasma proteomics, clinical measures from perfusion PET imaging, and PRS to evaluate their contributions to CMVD risk prediction in comprehensive machine and deep learning models. We then developed a novel unsupervised endotyping framework for CMVD from perfusion PET-derived myocardial blood flow data, revealing distinct patient subgroups beyond traditional case-control definitions. This imaging-based stratification substantially improved classification performance alongside plasma proteomics and PRS, achieving AUROCs between 0.65 and 0.73 per class, significantly outperforming binary classifiers and existing clinical models, highlighting the potential of this stratification approach to enable more precise and personalized diagnosis by capturing the underlying heterogeneity of CMVD. This work represents the first application of imaging-based endotyping and the integration of genetic and proteomic data for CMVD risk prediction, establishing a framework for multimodal modeling in complex diseases.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,UNCERTAIN,EC2_no_text_component,paper_type_unclear,unclear,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract focuses on risk prediction models using imaging, proteomics, and polygenic risk scores, with no natural-language text component or generative mechanism. Both reviewers agree on absence of text component (EC2) and generative model (EC3); EC2 is chosen as the primary exclusion.","No text/language component; the study integrates imaging, proteomics, and genetic data for risk prediction without any natural-language text or text-bio alignment.","The abstract focuses on risk prediction models using machine/deep learning and integration of imaging, proteomics, and polygenic risk scores; no generative mechanism (decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, etc.) is described.","The abstract focuses on risk prediction models using imaging, proteomics, and polygenic risk scores, with no natural-language text component or generative mechanism. Both reviewers agree on absence of text component (EC2) and generative model (EC3); EC2 is chosen as the primary exclusion.",Integrating Imaging-Derived Clinical Endotypes with Plasma Proteomics and External Polygenic Risk Scores Enhances Coronary Microvascular Disease Risk Prediction.,"Coronary microvascular disease (CMVD) is an underdiagnosed but significant contributor to the burden of ischemic heart disease, characterized by angina and myocardial infarction. The development of risk prediction models such as polygenic risk scores (PRS) for CMVD has been limited by a lack of large-scale genome-wide association studies (GWAS). However, there is significant overlap between CMVD and enrollment criteria for coronary artery disease (CAD) GWAS. In this study, we developed CMVD PRS models by selecting variants identified in a CMVD GWAS and applying weights from an external CAD GWAS, using CMVD-associated loci as proxies for the genetic risk. We integrated plasma proteomics, clinical measures from perfusion PET imaging, and PRS to evaluate their contributions to CMVD risk prediction in comprehensive machine and deep learning models. We then developed a novel unsupervised endotyping framework for CMVD from perfusion PET-derived myocardial blood flow data, revealing distinct patient subgroups beyond traditional case-control definitions. This imaging-based stratification substantially improved classification performance alongside plasma proteomics and PRS, achieving AUROCs between 0.65 and 0.73 per class, significantly outperforming binary classifiers and existing clinical models, highlighting the potential of this stratification approach to enable more precise and personalized diagnosis by capturing the underlying heterogeneity of CMVD. This work represents the first application of imaging-based endotyping and the integration of genetic and proteomic data for CMVD risk prediction, establishing a framework for multimodal modeling in complex diseases.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes risk prediction models using imaging, proteomics, and genetic data with no natural-language text component or generative mechanism, so it fails EC2 and EC3.","The paper develops risk prediction models using imaging, proteomics, and genetic data; no text/language component or text-bio bridge is described, so it does not meet inclusion criteria for generative text-bio foundational models.","The abstract describes risk prediction models for CMVD using machine/deep learning, with no mention of generative architecture, text generation, or biological sequence generation. Therefore, it does not meet the criteria for generative text-bio foundational model.","The abstract describes risk prediction models using imaging, proteomics, and genetic data with no natural-language text component or generative mechanism, so it fails EC2 and EC3.",Integrating Imaging-Derived Clinical Endotypes with Plasma Proteomics and External Polygenic Risk Scores Enhances Coronary Microvascular Disease Risk Prediction.,"Coronary microvascular disease (CMVD) is an underdiagnosed but significant 