nerative mechanism; it is a predictive classification task, not generative.","No natural-language text component or text-bio bridge; the paper integrates imaging, proteomics, and genetic data for risk prediction using machine learning models.","The abstract describes risk prediction models using machine/deep learning and polygenic risk scores, with no mention of a generative mechanism; it is a predictive classification task, not generative.",,Integrating Imaging-Derived Clinical Endotypes with Plasma Proteomics and External Polygenic Risk Scores Enhances Coronary Microvascular Disease Risk Prediction.,"Coronary microvascular disease (CMVD) is an underdiagnosed but significant contributor to the burden of ischemic heart disease, characterized by angina and myocardial infarction. The development of risk prediction models such as polygenic risk scores (PRS) for CMVD has been limited by a lack of large-scale genome-wide association studies (GWAS). However, there is significant overlap between CMVD and enrollment criteria for coronary artery disease (CAD) GWAS. In this study, we developed CMVD PRS models by selecting variants identified in a CMVD GWAS and applying weights from an external CAD GWAS, using CMVD-associated loci as proxies for the genetic risk. We integrated plasma proteomics, clinical measures from perfusion PET imaging, and PRS to evaluate their contributions to CMVD risk prediction in comprehensive machine and deep learning models. We then developed a novel unsupervised endotyping framework for CMVD from perfusion PET-derived myocardial blood flow data, revealing distinct patient subgroups beyond traditional case-control definitions. This imaging-based stratification substantially improved classification performance alongside plasma proteomics and PRS, achieving AUROCs between 0.65 and 0.73 per class, significantly outperforming binary classifiers and existing clinical models, highlighting the potential of this stratification approach to enable more precise and personalized diagnosis by capturing the underlying heterogeneity of CMVD. This work represents the first application of imaging-based endotyping and the integration of genetic and proteomic data for CMVD risk prediction, establishing a framework for multimodal modeling in complex diseases.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The abstract details imaging, proteomics, and polygenic risk score integration but contains no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping, so the text component and bridge are absent. | The abstract reports risk prediction and unsupervised endotyping with no mention of generative mechanisms such as decoder or autoregressive generation; therefore it lacks generative model evidence and foundation-model characteristics.","The abstract details imaging, proteomics, and polygenic risk score integration but contains no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping, so the text component and bridge are absent.",The abstract reports risk prediction and unsupervised endotyping with no mention of generative mechanisms such as decoder or autoregressive generation; therefore it lacks generative model evidence and foundation-model characteristics.,,Integrating Imaging-Derived Clinical Endotypes with Plasma Proteomics and External Polygenic Risk Scores Enhances Coronary Microvascular Disease Risk Prediction.,"Coronary microvascular disease (CMVD) is an underdiagnosed but significant contributor to the burden of ischemic heart disease, characterized by angina and myocardial infarction. The development of risk prediction models such as polygenic risk scores (PRS) for CMVD has been limited by a lack of large-scale genome-wide association studies (GWAS). However, there is significant overlap between CMVD and enrollment criteria for coronary artery disease (CAD) GWAS. In this study, we developed CMVD PRS models by selecting variants identified in a CMVD GWAS and applying weights from an external CAD GWAS, using CMVD-associated loci as proxies for the genetic risk. We integrated plasma proteomics, clinical measures from perfusion PET imaging, and PRS to evaluate their contributions to CMVD risk prediction in comprehensive machine and deep learning models. We then developed a novel unsupervised endotyping framework for CMVD from perfusion PET-derived myocardial blood flow data, revealing distinct patient subgroups beyond traditional case-control definitions. This imaging-based stratification substantially improved classification performance alongside plasma proteomics and PRS, achieving AUROCs between 0.65 and 0.73 per class, significantly outperforming binary classifiers and existing clinical models, highlighting the potential of this stratification approach to enable more precise and personalized diagnosis by capturing the underlying heterogeneity of CMVD. This work represents the first application of imaging-based endotyping and the integration of genetic and proteomic data for CMVD risk prediction, establishing a framework for multimodal modeling in complex diseases.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The abstract describes integration of imaging, proteomics, and polygenic risk scores for risk prediction, with no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping. | The abstract focuses on risk prediction, classification, and unsupervised endotyping without any reference to generative mechanisms such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion models.","The abstract describes integration of imaging, proteomics, and polygenic risk scores for risk prediction, with no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio mapping.","The abstract focuses on risk prediction, classification, and unsupervised endotyping without any reference to generative mechanisms such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion models.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:148:331,Integrating Prior Knowledge Using Transformer for Gene Regulatory Network Inference.,"Gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, a process of reconstructing gene regulatory rules from experimental data, has the potential to discover new regulatory rules. However, existing methods often struggle to generalize across diverse cell types and account for unseen regulators. Here, this work presents GRNPT, a novel Transformer-based framework that integrates large language model (LLM) embeddings from publicly accessible biological data and a temporal convolutional network (TCN) autoencoder to capture regulatory patterns from single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) trajectories. GRNPT significantly outperforms both supervised and unsupervised methods in inferring GRNs, particularly when training data is limited. Notably, GRNPT exhibits exceptional generalizability, accurately predicting regulatory relationships in previously unseen cell types and even regulators. By combining LLMs ability to distillate biological knowledge from text and deep learning methodologies capturing complex patterns in gene expression data, GRNPT overcomes the limitations of traditional GRN inference methods and enables more accurate and comprehensive understanding of gene regulatory dynamics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Integrating Prior Knowledge Using Transformer for Gene Regulatory Network Inference.,"Gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, a process of reconstructing gene regulatory rules from experimental data, has the potential to discover new regulatory rules. However, existing methods often struggle to generalize across diverse cell types and account for unseen regulators. Here, this work presents GRNPT, a novel Transformer-based framework that integrates large language model (LLM) embeddings from publicly accessible biological data and a temporal convolutional network (TCN) autoencoder to capture regulatory patterns from single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) trajectories. GRNPT significantly outperforms both supervised and unsupervised methods in inferring GRNs, particularly when training data is limited. Notably, GRNPT exhibits exceptional generalizability, accurately predicting regulatory relationships in previously unseen cell types and even regulators. By combining LLMs ability to distillate biological knowledge from text and deep learning methodologies capturing complex patterns in gene expression data, GRNPT overcomes the limitations of traditional GRN inference methods and enables more accurate and comprehensive understanding of gene regulatory dynamics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,none,"LLM embeddings used as prior, no explicit text-bio alignment","GRN inference, no generative mechanism described",wrapper_boundary,"GRNPT uses frozen LLM embeddings as prior knowledge for gene regulatory network inference from scRNA-seq data, making it an application_wrapper. No generative or explicit text-bio conditioning is present.","GRNPT uses LLM embeddings as side features for GRN inference from scRNA-seq, not as a generative text-bio model or substantive text component; therefore it is an application wrapper.","The abstract describes a Transformer-based framework for gene regulatory network inference, a predictive task, with no mention of generative mechanisms such as autoregressive, decoder, or sequence generation. The use of LLM embeddings does not make the candidate model generative.","GRNPT uses frozen LLM embeddings as prior knowledge for gene regulatory network inference from scRNA-seq data, making it an application_wrapper. No generative or explicit text-bio conditioning is present.",Integrating Prior Knowledge Using Transformer for Gene Regulatory Network Inference.,"Gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, a process of reconstructing gene regulatory rules from experimental data, has the potential to discover new regulatory rules. However, existing methods often struggle to generalize across diverse cell types and account for unseen regulators. Here, this work presents GRNPT, a novel Transformer-based framework that integrates large language model (LLM) embeddings from publicly accessible biological data and a temporal convolutional network (TCN) autoencoder to capture regulatory patterns from single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) trajectories. GRNPT significantly outperforms both supervised and unsupervised methods in inferring GRNs, particularly when training data is limited. Notably, GRNPT exhibits exceptional generalizability, accurately predicting regulatory relationships in previously unseen cell types and even regulators. By combining LLMs ability to distillate biological knowledge from text and deep learning methodologies capturing complex patterns in gene expression data, GRNPT overcomes the limitations of traditional GRN inference methods and enables more accurate and comprehensive understanding of gene regulatory dynamics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,unclear,none,none,none,wrapper_boundary,"GRNPT uses frozen LLM embeddings as a prior for a downstream GRN inference predictor, with no natural-language text input/output or generative mechanism, making it an application_wrapper.","GRNPT uses external LLM embeddings as features for a downstream GRN inference task, which is an application wrapper; no substantive text component or text-bio bridge in the candidate model itself.","The abstract describes GRNPT as a Transformer-based framework for gene regulatory network inference (predicting regulatory relationships), not a generative model. No generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion is mentioned.","GRNPT uses frozen LLM embeddings as a prior for a downstream GRN inference predictor, with no natural-language text input/output or generative mechanism, making it an application_wrapper.",Integrating Prior Knowledge Using Transformer for Gene Regulatory Network Inference.,"Gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, a process of reconstructing gene regulatory rules from experimental data, has the potential to discover new regulatory rules. However, existing methods often struggle to generalize across diverse cell types and account for unseen regulators. Here, this work presents GRNPT, a novel Transformer-based framework that integrates large language model (LLM) embeddings from publicly accessible biological data and a temporal convolutional network (TCN) autoencoder to capture regulatory patterns from single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) trajectories. GRNPT significantly outperforms both supervised and unsupervised methods in inferring GRNs, particularly when training data is limited. Notably, GRNPT exhibits exceptional generalizability, accurately predicting regulatory relationships in previously unseen cell types and even regulators. By combining LLMs ability to distillate biological knowledge from text and deep learning methodologies capturing complex patterns in gene expression data, GRNPT overcomes the limitations of traditional GRN inference methods and enables more accurate and comprehensive understanding of gene regulatory dynamics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,none,none,GRNPT ... to capture regulatory patterns ... inference,wrapper_boundary,"The abstract describes GRNPT as a framework that integrates LLM embeddings for gene regulatory network inference, not a generative model. No natural-language text input/output or text-bio alignment is present; the LLM embeddings are used as prior knowledge for a downstream prediction task, making it an application_wrapper.","GRNPT uses LLM embeddings as side features for GRN inference, not as a generative text-bio model; it is an application wrapper around an existing LLM.","The paper describes GRNPT, a framework for gene regulatory network inference using LLM embeddings and a TCN autoencoder, but it is a predictive/classification task (inferring regulatory relationships) with no generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive generation, or diffusion.","The abstract describes GRNPT as a framework that integrates LLM embeddings for gene regulatory network inference, not a generative model. No natural-language text input/output or text-bio alignment is present; the LLM embeddings are used as prior knowledge for a downstream prediction task, making it an application_wrapper.",Integrating Prior Knowledge Using Transformer for Gene Regulatory Network Inference.,"Gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, a process of reconstructing gene regulatory rules from experimental data, has the potential to discover new regulatory rules. However, existing methods often struggle to generalize across diverse cell types and account for unseen regulators. Here, this work presents GRNPT, a novel Transformer-based framework that integrates large language model (LLM) embeddings from publicly accessible biological data and a temporal convolutional network (TCN) autoencoder to capture regulatory patterns from single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) trajectories. GRNPT significantly outperforms both supervised and unsupervised methods in inferring GRNs, particularly when training data is limited. Notably, GRNPT exhibits exceptional generalizability, accurately predicting regulatory relationships in previously unseen cell types and even regulators. By combining LLMs ability to distillate biological knowledge from text and deep learning methodologies capturing complex patterns in gene expression data, GRNPT overcomes the limitations of traditional GRN inference methods and enables more accurate and comprehensive understanding of gene regulatory dynamics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,yes,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a novel transformer‑based GRN inference model that incorporates LLM embeddings but provides no natural‑language text input, output, or generation. Consequently there is no text component or generative model, leading to exclusion under EC2_no_text_component.","The work presents GRNPT, a new framework that incorporates LLM embeddings as prior knowledge for scRNA‑seq based GRN inference; the text usage is limited to embeddings and does not constitute a substantive text component or bridge, so it is treated as an application wrapper.","The abstract introduces a novel Transformer-based framework for GRN inference, showing no generative mechanism, while explicitly leveraging large language model embeddings, indicating foundation model use.","The abstract describes a novel transformer‑based GRN inference model that incorporates LLM embeddings but provides no natural‑language text input, output, or generation. Consequently there is no text component or generative model, leading to exclusion under EC2_no_text_component.",Integrating Prior Knowledge Using Transformer for Gene Regulatory Network Inference.,"Gene regulatory network (GRN) inference, a process of reconstructing gene regulatory rules from experimental data, has the potential to discover new regulatory rules. However, existing methods often struggle to generalize across diverse cell types and account for unseen regulators. Here, this work presents GRNPT, a novel Transformer-based framework that integrates large language model (LLM) embeddings from publicly accessible biological data and a temporal convolutional network (TCN) autoencoder to capture regulatory patterns from single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) trajectories. GRNPT significantly outperforms both supervised and unsupervised methods in inferring GRNs, particularly when training data is limited. Notably, GRNPT exhibits exceptional generalizability, accurately predicting regulatory relationships in previously unseen cell types and even regulators. By combining LLMs ability to distillate biological knowledge from text and deep learning methodologies capturing complex patterns in gene expression data, GRNPT overcomes the limitations of traditional GRN inference methods and enables more accurate and comprehensive understanding of gene regulatory dynamics.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,yes,none,none,none,clear_exclude,The abstract describes a Transformer model that leverages LLM embeddings as side information for GRN inference without generating text or providing a multimodal text‑bio generation; thus it is an application‑wrapper paper and excluded.,"The work presents a novel Transformer framework that integrates LLM embeddings as side information for GRN inference, which is a downstream predictive task; embeddings alone do not constitute a substantive natural-language text component or bridge, so it is an application wrapper.","The abstract presents a novel Transformer framework for GRN inference without mentioning any decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism (generative_model_present=no). It explicitly integrates large language model embeddings, indicating use of a foundation model (foundation_model_evidence=yes).",The abstract describes a Transformer model that leverages LLM embeddings as side information for GRN inference without generating text or providing a multimodal text‑bio generation; thus it is an application‑wrapper paper and excluded.
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:149:945,Integration of multi-modal datasets to estimate human aging,"{'h1': 'Abstract', 'p': 'Aging involves complex biological processes leading to the decline of living organisms. As population lifespan increases worldwide, the importance of identifying factors underlying healthy aging has become critical. Integration of multi-modal datasets is a powerful approach for the analysis of complex biological systems, with the potential to uncover novel aging biomarkers. In this study, we leveraged publicly available epigenomic, transcriptomic and telomere length data along with histological images from the Genotype-Tissue Expression project to build tissue-specific regression models for age prediction. Using data from two tissues, lung and ovary, we aimed to compare model performance across data modalities, as well as to assess the improvement resulting from integrating multiple data types. Our results demostrate that methylation outperformed the other data modalities, with a mean absolute error of 3.36 and 4.36 in the test sets for lung and ovary, respectively. These models achieved lower error rates when compared with established state-of-the-art tissue-agnostic methylation models, emphasizing the importance of a tissue-specific approach. Additionally, this work has shown how the application of Hierarchical Image Pyramid Transformers for feature extraction significantly enhances age modeling using histological images. Finally, we evaluated the benefits of integrating multiple data modalities into a single model. Combining methylation data with other data modalities only marginally improved performance likely due to the limited number of available samples. Combining gene expression with histological features yielded more accurate age predictions compared with the individual performance of these data types. Given these results, this study shows how machine learning applications can be extended to/in multi-modal aging research. Code used is available at https://github.com/zroger49/multi_modal_age_prediction .'}",exclude_uncertain_unstable,Integration of multi-modal datasets to estimate human aging,"{'h1': 'Abstract', 'p': 'Aging involves complex biological processes leading to the decline of living organisms. As population lifespan increases worldwide, the importance of identifying factors underlying healthy aging has become critical. Integration of multi-modal datasets is a powerful approach for the analysis of complex biological systems, with the potential to uncover novel aging biomarkers. In this study, we leveraged publicly available epigenomic, transcriptomic and telomere length data along with histological images from the Genotype-Tissue Expression project to build tissue-specific regression models for age prediction. Using data from two tissues, lung and ovary, we aimed to compare model performance across data modalities, as well as to assess the improvement resulting from integrating multiple data types. Our results demostrate that methylation outperformed the other data modalities, with a mean absolute error of 3.36 and 4.36 in the test sets for lung and ovary, respectively. These models achieved lower error rates when compared with established state-of-the-art tissue-agnostic methylation models, emphasizing the importance of a tissue-specific approach. Additionally, this work has shown how the application of Hierarchical Image Pyramid Transformers for feature extraction significantly enhances age modeling using histological images. Finally, we evaluated the benefits of integrating multiple data modalities into a single model. Combining methylation data with other data modalities only marginally improved performance likely due to the limited number of available samples. Combining gene expression with histological features yielded more accurate age predictions compared with the individual performance of these data types. Given these resul