e across multiple time points and remained independent of conventional clinical indicators. Mechanistically, we identified a subset of STAB1^+ tumor-associated macrophages (TAMs) enriched in COAD tumors, exhibiting enhanced efferocytosis activity, M2-like polarization, and mTORC1 signaling activation. In vitro, STAB1 expression was essential for IL-4-induced M2 polarization, and its inhibition attenuated the formation of immunosuppressive TAMs. Single-cell and spatial transcriptomic analyses revealed that this macrophage population was transcriptionally distinct and increased in abundance following neoadjuvant therapy. Conclusion This study establishes a multimodal prognostic system that integrates histopathological imaging with molecular profiling, and for the first time reveals the pivotal role of STAB1^+ TAMs in orchestrating the immunosuppressive TME via efferocytosis and mTORC1 activation. Our findings provide both a clinically applicable risk assessment tool and a potential therapeutic target. Targeting STAB1 may broaden the benefit of immunotherapy for COAD patients with limited responses to immune checkpoint blockade. Graphical Abstract",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,no,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a prognostic risk model integrating histopathology and transcriptomics using ResNet50 and machine learning, with no text component, no text-bio bridge, and no generative mechanism; it is an application wrapper, not a generative text-bio foundational model.","The abstract describes a prognostic model integrating histopathology and transcriptomics, with no mention of any text/language component or natural language processing. Therefore, it does not satisfy the text component criterion.","The abstract describes a prognostic risk model using deep learning on histopathology and transcriptomics, with no mention of generative mechanisms such as decoder, autoregressive, or sequence generation."