t component or generative mechanism is described, leading to exclusion under EC2_no_text_component and EC3_not_generative.",Multimodal AI for prediction of distant metastasis in carcinoma patients.,"Metastasis of cancer is directly related to death in almost all cases, however a lot is yet to be understood about this process. Despite advancements in the available radiological investigation techniques, not all cases of Distant Metastasis (DM) are diagnosed at initial clinical presentation. Also, there are currently no standard biomarkers of metastasis. Early, accurate diagnosis of DM is however crucial for clinical decision making, and planning of appropriate management strategies. Previous works have achieved little success in attempts to predict DM from either clinical, genomic, radiology, or histopathology data. In this work we attempt a multimodal approach to predict the presence of DM in cancer patients by combining gene expression data, clinical data and histopathology images. We tested a novel combination of Random Forest (RF) algorithm with an optimization technique for gene selection, and investigated if gene expression pattern in the primary tissues of three cancer types (Bladder Carcinoma, Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma, and Head and Neck Squamous Carcinoma) with DM are similar or different. Gene expression biomarkers of DM identified by our proposed method outperformed Differentially Expressed Genes (DEGs) identified by the DESeq2 software package in the task of predicting presence or absence of DM. Genes involved in DM tend to be more cancer type specific rather than general across all cancers. Our results also indicate that multimodal data is more predictive of metastasis than either of the three unimodal data tested, and genomic data provides the highest contribution by a wide margin. The results re-emphasize the importance for availability of sufficient image data when a weakly supervised training technique is used. Code is made available at: https://github.com/rit-cui-lab/Multimodal-AI-for-Prediction-of-Distant-Metastasis-in-Carcinoma-Patients.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The paper describes a multimodal prediction model using gene expression, clinical data, and histopathology images for metastasis prediction, with no natural-language text component or text-bio bridge, and it is not a generative model. | The paper presents a Random Forest-based predictive model for distant metastasis classification; no generative mechanism or foundation model evidence is described.","The paper describes a multimodal prediction model using gene expression, clinical data, and histopathology images for metastasis prediction, with no natural-language text component or text-bio bridge, and it is not a generative model.",The paper presents a Random Forest-based predictive model for distant metastasis classification; no generative mechanism or foundation model evidence is described.,,Multimodal AI for prediction of distant metastasis in carcinoma patients.,"Metastasis of cancer is directly related to death in almost all cases, however a lot is yet to be understood about this process. Despite advancements in the available radiological investigation techniques, not all cases of Distant Metastasis (DM) are diagnosed at initial clinical presentation. Also, there are currently no standard biomarkers of metastasis. Early, accurate diagnosis of DM is however crucial for clinical decision making, and planning of appropriate management strategies. Previous works have achieved little success in attempts to predict DM from either clinical, genomic, radiology, or histopathology data. In this work we attempt a multimodal approach to predict the presence of DM in cancer patients by combining gene expression data, clinical data and histopathology images. We tested a novel combination of Random Forest (RF) algorithm with an optimization technique for gene selection, and investigated if gene expression pattern in the primary tissues of three cancer types (Bladder Carcinoma, Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma, and Head and Neck Squamous Carcinoma) with DM are similar or different. Gene expression biomarkers of DM identified by our proposed method outperformed Differentially Expressed Genes (DEGs) identified by the DESeq2 software package in the task of predicting presence or absence of DM. Genes involved in DM tend to be more cancer type specific rather than general across all cancers. Our results also indicate that multimodal data is more predictive of metastasis than either of the three unimodal data tested, and genomic data provides the highest contribution by a wide margin. The results re-emphasize the importance for availability of sufficient image data when a weakly supervised training technique is used. Code is made available at: https://github.com/rit-cui-lab/Multimodal-AI-for-Prediction-of-Distant-Metastasis-in-Carcinoma-Patients.",exclude_uncertain_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,no,no,,,,,"The abstract presents a multimodal predictive model using gene expression, clinical data, and histopathology images, with no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio bridge. | The abstract focuses on predicting distant metastasis with a Random Forest multimodal model and does not mention any generative architecture or decoder.","The abstract presents a multimodal predictive model using gene expression, clinical data, and histopathology images, with no mention of natural-language text or a text‑bio bridge.",The abstract focuses on predicting distant metastasis with a Random Forest multimodal model and does not mention any generative architecture or decoder.,,Multimodal AI for prediction of distant metastasis in carcinoma patients.,"Metastasis of cancer is directly related to death in almost all cases, however a lot is yet to be understood about this process. Despite advancements in the available radiological investigation techniques, not all cases of Distant Metastasis (DM) are diagnosed at initial clinical presentation. Also, there are currently no standard biomarkers of metastasis. Early, accurate diagnosis of DM is however crucial for clinical decision making, and planning of appropriate management strategies. Previous wort":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract is about multimodal oncology data integration and foundation-model-driven dataset generation, but it does not describe a substantive text or language component. That makes it outside the text-bio scope.","evidence_snippet":"\"Embedding-Based Deep Learning Frameworks for Multimodal Oncology Data Integration\"; \"foundation model-driven approach to generating multimodal oncology datasets\""},{"record_id":"rec_003261","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title explicitly says 'a survey' and the abstract says it 'comprehensively reviews' LLM applications in biomedical data analysis. This is a review rather than a primary model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"The large language models on biomedical data analysis: a survey\"; \"This comprehensive review\""},{"record_id":"rec_003262","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract repeatedly frames the paper as a comprehensive review of LLMs in bioinformatics and 'systematically summarize[s] the current research status'. That is clearly review/editorial.","evidence_snippet":"\"This comprehensive review systematically examines...\""},{"record_id":"rec_003263","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title says 'A Survey of Multimodal Large Language Models in Biomedical Engineering and Healthcare', so this is a review paper rather than a primary model contribution.","evidence_snippet":"\"A Survey of Multimodal Large Language Models in Biomedical Engineering and Healthcare\""},{"record_id":"rec_003264","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract presents a new foundational agent for multi-omics interpretation with explicit reasoning and latent embeddings, but it does not describe a text or language component. It is therefore outside the generative text-bio scope.","evidence_snippet":"\"We introduce BiOmics, a foundational agent that synthesizes multi-omics data\"; \"explicit reasoning space\""}]}
