./data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0002.stderr.log:108:[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_000246","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_000246","source_record_id":"rec_000246","source_corpus":"include","title":"Cross-species modeling of plant genomes at single-nucleotide resolution using a pretrained DNA language model.","abstract":"","doi":"10.1073/pnas.2421738122","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[discussion_conclusion: Discussion]\nFunctional annotation of plant genomes is crucial for plant genomics and crop breeding but remains limited by the lack of functional genomic data and accurate predictive models. Here, we introduced PlantCaduceus, a multispecies plant DNA LM pretrained on a curated set of 16 evolutionarily distant angiosperm genomes, enabling cross -species prediction of functional annotations with limited data. PlantCaduceus leverages Mamba ( 24 ) and Caduceus ( 23 ) architectures to support bidirectional, RC equivariant sequence modeling. We demonstrated the superior cross -species performance of PlantCaduceus on five tasks involving transcription, translation, and evolutionary constraint modeling. These results highlight the potential of PlantCaduceus to serve as a foundational model for comprehensively understanding plant genomes.\n\nPlantCaduceus has the potential to accurately annotate any newly sequenced angiosperm genomes. Unlike supervised deep learning models that easily overfit on limited labeled data, PlantCaduceus demonstrates robust cross -species performance in modeling transcription, translation, and evolutionary constraints, even for species not included in pretraining ( Fig. 2 and SI Appendix , Fig. S2 ). This indicates that through self -supervised pretraining on large -scale genomic datasets, PlantCaduceus has captured broad evolutionary conservation and DNA sequence grammar. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Methods]\nPretraining Dataset. The pretraining dataset comprises 16 genomes from two distinct clades: eight genomes from the family Poaceae and eight genomes from the order Brassicales (Dataset S1). To visualize their relatedness, we subset these taxa from a large phylogeny of seed plants (58). The Poaceae species displayed substantial variation in genome size and repetitive sequence content, with the hexaploid wheat genome exhibiting a size of 15 Gbp. For each Poaceae genome, except for Tripsacum, we obtained the genome and corresponding genome annotation and repeat -masked annotation from the Joint Genome Institute. For the Tripsacum genome, the genome FASTA and annotation files were downloaded from MaizeGDB (https://maizegdb.org/genome/assembly/ Td -FL\\_9056069\\_6 -DRAFT -PanAnd -1.0), and the EDTA tool (59) was used to identify repetitive sequences within the genome. Based on the repeat -masked annotation, each genome was softmasked with bedtools (60) and subsequently divided into genomic windows of 512 bp with a step size of 256 bp. Each window was assigned to a unique class based on the genome annotation, and all coding sequence regions were selected for pretraining. The remaining genomic regions were then down -sampled to ensure an equal number of CDS regions and noncoding regions. It is important to note that for the hexaploid wheat genome, only subgenome A was utilized to avoid species bias. The Brassicales genomes datasets were acquired from the GPN (21) Hugging Face repository (https://huggingface. [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"Discussion","heading_level":3,"line_start":137,"line_end":156,"text":"Functional annotation of plant genomes is crucial for plant genomics and crop breeding but remains limited by the lack of functional genomic data and accurate predictive models. Here, we introduced PlantCaduceus, a multispecies plant DNA LM pretrained on a curated set of 16 evolutionarily distant angiosperm genomes, enabling cross -species prediction of functional annotations with limited data. PlantCaduceus leverages Mamba ( 24 ) and Caduceus ( 23 ) architectures to support bidirectional, RC equivariant sequence modeling. We demonstrated the superior cross -species performance of PlantCaduceus on five tasks involving transcription, translation, and evolutionary constraint modeling. These results highlight the potential of PlantCaduceus to serve as a foundational model for comprehensively understanding plant genomes.\n\nPlantCaduceus has the potential to accurately annotate any newly sequenced angiosperm genomes. Unlike supervised deep learning models that easily overfit on limited labeled data, PlantCaduceus demonstrates robust cross -species performance in modeling transcription, translation, and evolutionary constraints, even for species not included in pretraining ( Fig. 2 and SI Appendix , Fig. S2 ). This indicates that through self -supervised pretraining on large -scale genomic datasets, PlantCaduceus has captured broad evolutionary conservation and DNA sequence grammar. [truncated]","original_chars":7003},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Methods","heading_level":3,"line_start":157,"line_end":281,"text":"Pretraining Dataset. The pretraining dataset comprises 16 genomes from two distinct clades: eight genomes from the family Poaceae and eight genomes from the order Brassicales (Dataset S1). To visualize their relatedness, we subset these taxa from a large phylogeny of seed plants (58). The Poaceae species displayed substantial variation in genome size and repetitive sequence content, with the hexaploid wheat genome exhibiting a size of 15 Gbp. For each Poaceae genome, except for Tripsacum, we obtained the genome and corresponding genome annotation and repeat -masked annotation from the Joint Genome Institute. For the Tripsacum genome, the genome FASTA and annotation files were downloaded from MaizeGDB (https://maizegdb.org/genome/assembly/ Td -FL\\_9056069\\_6 -DRAFT -PanAnd -1.0), and the EDTA tool (59) was used to identify repetitive sequences within the genome. Based on the repeat -masked annotation, each genome was softmasked with bedtools (60) and subsequently divided into genomic windows of 512 bp with a step size of 256 bp. Each window was assigned to a unique class based on the genome annotation, and all coding sequence regions were selected for pretraining. The remaining genomic regions were then down -sampled to ensure an equal number of CDS regions and noncoding regions. It is important to note that for the hexaploid wheat genome, only subgenome A was utilized to avoid species bias. The Brassicales genomes datasets were acquired from the GPN (21) Hugging Face repository (https://huggingface. [truncated]","original_chars":31847}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_000246.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_000246","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary model paper on plant DNA sequence modeling, but the provided text describes genomic sequence pretraining and cross-species functional annotation, not natural-language text or a text-bio bridge. The text component criterion is not met because the evidence is biological sequence only.","evidence_snippet":"\"a multispecies plant DNA LM pretrained on a curated set of 16 evolutionarily distant angiosperm genomes\"; \"pretraining dataset comprises 16 genomes\"; \"captured broad evolutionary conservation and DNA sequence grammar\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_000246","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record describes a pretrained plant DNA language model with self-supervised pretraining on 16 genomes and broad cross-species transfer. It does not explicitly state a generative mechanism beyond sequence modeling and prediction tasks.","evidence_snippet":"\"multispecies plant DNA LM pretrained on a curated set of 16 evolutionarily distant angiosperm genomes\"; \"self-supervised pretraining on large-scale genomic datasets\"; \"cross-species prediction of functional annotations\"; \"broad evolutionary conservation and DNA sequence grammar\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]
