./data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0060.stderr.log:108:[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_002329","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_002329","source_record_id":"rec_002329","source_corpus":"uncertain","title":"Biologically-Informed Hybrid Membership Inference Attacks on Generative Genomic Models","abstract":"The increased availability of genetic data has transformed genomics research, but raised many privacy concerns regarding its handling due to its sensitive nature. This work explores the use of language models (LMs) for the generation of synthetic genetic mutation profiles, leveraging differential privacy (DP) for the protection of sensitive genetic data. We empirically evaluate the privacy guarantees of our DP modes by introducing a novel Biologically-Informed Hybrid Membership Inference Attack (biHMIA), which combines traditional black box MIA with contextual genomics metrics for enhanced attack power. Our experiments show that both small and large transformer GPT-like models are viable synthetic variant generators for small-scale genomics, and that our hybrid attack leads, on average, to higher adversarial success compared to traditional metric-based MIAs.","doi":"10.48550/arXiv.2511.07503","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nThe increased availability of genetic data has transformed genomics research, but raised many privacy concerns regarding its handling due to its sensitive nature. This work explores the use of language models (LMs) for the generation of synthetic genetic mutation profiles, leveraging differential privacy (DP) for the protection of sensitive genetic data. We empirically evaluate the privacy guarantees of our DP modes by introducing a novel Biologically-Informed Hybrid Membership Inference Attack (biHMIA), which combines traditional black box MIA with contextual genomics metrics for enhanced attack power. Our experiments show that both small and large transformer GPT-like models are viable synthetic variant generators for small-scale genomics, and that our hybrid attack leads, on average, to higher adversarial success compared to traditional metric-based MIAs.\n\n[introduction: Introduction]\nGenomics is the study of the structure and function of an organism's genetic information: all humans share over 99% of their DNA, and less than 1% of the entire genetic information dictates the unique variety of human observable traits. Differences between individuals' DNA, known as genetic mutations , most commonly comprise of changes (deletions, insertions and substitutions, or Single Nucleotide Polymorphisms (SNP)) of nucleic bases called alleles , in specific genomic location (or loci ) compared to the reference human genome. The 'rarity' of a specific allele is measured via Allele Frequency (AF) or Minor Allele Frequency (MAF), as the prevalence of the rarest occurring base within all alleles in the study cohort, with mutations being classified as rare if they appear in less than 0.5-1% of the population. In the early 2000s, the efforts of the Human Genome Project (HGP) (Collins, Morgan, and Patrinos 2003) led to the publishing of the first sequenced human genome, and represented the very beginning of the Genomic Era. In the following years, as sequencing costs decreased due to the development of high-throughput technologies and tools (Gauthier et al. 2019), the amount of available genetic data saw an exorbitant increase, pushing the birth of major public international bioinformatics initiatives like the 1000 Genomes\n\nCopyright © 2026, Association for the Advancement of Artificial Intelligence (www.aaai.org). All rights reserved.\n\nProject (Auton et al. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Discussion]\n#### Innate Privacy and Differential Privacy Effects\n\nWe speculate that the noise addition introduced by DP acts as a regularizer for the model, reducing model hallucination, which we speculate may be responsible for the positional validity and quality increase in the DP-trained MinGPTgenerated sequences. To this we also attribute the increase in mutation uniqueness in the DP-trained MinGPT, speculating that that the noise addition increases the sampling randomness during the generation process leading to more stochastic generation tendencies.\n\n#### Results Variance\n\nAll attacks were subject to significant fluctuation between rounds on different randomly-chosen target cohorts. We experienced a non-insignificant level of incongruity between different hybrid MIA runs on the same data, particularly for the MinGPT models. We speculate that this is due to variations within samples generated on different runs (and, thus, variability among the extracted genomic features), which we attributed to the higher model stochasticity. This is further supported by the fact that we did not find the same high variability for GPT-2, which tended to produce more repetitive samples. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Conclusion]\nOur research explored the suitability of Language Models for the generation of synthetic sample-level genomic mutation profiles, leveraging Differential Privacy to enhance the models' privacy guarantees. We introduce a hybrid MIA attack which combines model metrics with genomicspecific features extracted from the generated samples to infer membership. This attack shows on average higher success rates compared to model-only based MIA, thus representing a promising solution for genomics-specific privacy assessments of generative models. We found that GPT-like transformer-based models are valid generators of synthetic genomic samples, both if trained from scratch and if finetuned. Smaller models tend to offer more inter-sample diversity within generated multi-sample cohorts, but at the cost of positional verisimilitude. We found that smaller transformers provide better innate privacy, with low memorization and good robustness against MIAs. Larger models, on the other hand, are more vulnerable to MIAs and tend to show high levels of memorization. Furthermore, implementing DP into the training pipeline increases the privacy robustness of the model to inference attacks, and produces a regularization effect that enhances the model's utility.\n\n#### Future Directions\n\nTraining or finetuning the models on larger more diverse datasets, like using mutations from all chromosomes or leveraging data from large biobanks (Sudlow et al. 2015), could lead to better utility. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Data and Preprocessing]\nWe use individual-level Variant Call Format (VCF) data of 2504 samples from the 1000 Genomes Project Phase 3 files (Auton et al. 2015). We focus on genetic mutations present on Chromosome 22 for all study samples in order to reduce computational overheads given its lower density of mutations, all while preserving the format and statistics of the data. To best allow the model to understand the underlying variants distributions in a sample-wise manner, we extracted individual genetic profiles from the source VCF file by querying the VCF dataset variant-wise (row-wise) and encoding each mutation as a string ' CHR : POS : REFs &gt; ALTs GT ' to introduce some context into the input, while keeping the amount of non-genomics related tokens to a minimum.\n\n[data_representation: Methods]\n#### Data and Preprocessing\n\nWe use individual-level Variant Call Format (VCF) data of 2504 samples from the 1000 Genomes Project Phase 3 files (Auton et al. 2015). We focus on genetic mutations present on Chromosome 22 for all study samples in order to reduce computational overheads given its lower density of mutations, all while preserving the format and statistics of the data. To best allow the model to understand the underlying variants distributions in a sample-wise manner, we extracted individual genetic profiles from the source VCF file by querying the VCF dataset variant-wise (row-wise) and encoding each mutation as a string ' CHR : POS : REFs &gt; ALTs GT ' to introduce some context into the input, while keeping the amount of non-genomics related tokens to a minimum.\n\n#### Models\n\nWe focused on generative models for Causal Language Modelling (CLM), as they learn to predict the next mutation in the sample profile based on the previously seen mutations, which we believed would allow for better contextualization and generation of biologically-plausible data. We finetuned OpenAI's GPT-2 Model (Radford et al. 2019) and trained an adaptation of MinGPT (Karpathy 2024b), a small, fast and interpretable reimplementation of the GPT-2 transformer that allows for full parameter and training customization. [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":5,"line_start":11,"line_end":14,"text":"The increased availability of genetic data has transformed genomics research, but raised many privacy concerns regarding its handling due to its sensitive nature. This work explores the use of language models (LMs) for the generation of synthetic genetic mutation profiles, leveraging differential privacy (DP) for the protection of sensitive genetic data. We empirically evaluate the privacy guarantees of our DP modes by introducing a novel Biologically-Informed Hybrid Membership Inference Attack (biHMIA), which combines traditional black box MIA with contextual genomics metrics for enhanced attack power. Our experiments show that both small and large transformer GPT-like models are viable synthetic variant generators for small-scale genomics, and that our hybrid attack leads, on average, to higher adversarial success compared to traditional metric-based MIAs.","original_chars":870},{"section_type":"introduction","heading":"Introduction","heading_level":3,"line_start":15,"line_end":36,"text":"Genomics is the study of the structure and function of an organism's genetic information: all humans share over 99% of their DNA, and less than 1% of the entire genetic information dictates the unique variety of human observable traits. Differences between individuals' DNA, known as genetic mutations , most commonly comprise of changes (deletions, insertions and substitutions, or Single Nucleotide Polymorphisms (SNP)) of nucleic bases called alleles , in specific genomic location (or loci ) compared to the reference human genome. The 'rarity' of a specific allele is measured via Allele Frequency (AF) or Minor Allele Frequency (MAF), as the prevalence of the rarest occurring base within all alleles in the study cohort, with mutations being classified as rare if they appear in less than 0.5-1% of the population. In the early 2000s, the efforts of the Human Genome Project (HGP) (Collins, Morgan, and Patrinos 2003) led to the publishing of the first sequenced human genome, and represented the very beginning of the Genomic Era. In the following years, as sequencing costs decreased due to the development of high-throughput technologies and tools (Gauthier et al. 2019), the amount of available genetic data saw an exorbitant increase, pushing the birth of major public international bioinformatics initiatives like the 1000 Genomes\n\nCopyright © 2026, Association for the Advancement of Artificial Intelligence (www.aaai.org). All rights reserved.\n\nProject (Auton et al. [truncated]","original_chars":5784},{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"Discussion","heading_level":3,"line_start":94,"line_end":107,"text":"#### Innate Privacy and Differential Privacy Effects\n\nWe speculate that the noise addition introduced by DP acts as a regularizer for the model, reducing model hallucination, which we speculate may be responsible for the positional validity and quality increase in the DP-trained MinGPTgenerated sequences. To this we also attribute the increase in mutation uniqueness in the DP-trained MinGPT, speculating that that the noise addition increases the sampling randomness during the generation process leading to more stochastic generation tendencies.\n\n#### Results Variance\n\nAll attacks were subject to significant fluctuation between rounds on different randomly-chosen target cohorts. We experienced a non-insignificant level of incongruity between different hybrid MIA runs on the same data, particularly for the MinGPT models. We speculate that this is due to variations within samples generated on different runs (and, thus, variability among the extracted genomic features), which we attributed to the higher model stochasticity. This is further supported by the fact that we did not find the same high variability for GPT-2, which tended to produce more repetitive samples. [truncated]","original_chars":2265},{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"Conclusion","heading_level":3,"line_start":108,"line_end":115,"text":"Our research explored the suitability of Language Models for the generation of synthetic sample-level genomic mutation profiles, leveraging Differential Privacy to enhance the models' privacy guarantees. We introduce a hybrid MIA attack which combines model metrics with genomicspecific features extracted from the generated samples to infer membership. This attack shows on average higher success rates compared to model-only based MIA, thus representing a promising solution for genomics-specific privacy assessments of generative models. We found that GPT-like transformer-based models are valid generators of synthetic genomic samples, both if trained from scratch and if finetuned. Smaller models tend to offer more inter-sample diversity within generated multi-sample cohorts, but at the cost of positional verisimilitude. We found that smaller transformers provide better innate privacy, with low memorization and good robustness against MIAs. Larger models, on the other hand, are more vulnerable to MIAs and tend to show high levels of memorization. Furthermore, implementing DP into the training pipeline increases the privacy robustness of the model to inference attacks, and produces a regularization effect that enhances the model's utility.\n\n#### Future Directions\n\nTraining or finetuning the models on larger more diverse datasets, like using mutations from all chromosomes or leveraging data from large biobanks (Sudlow et al. 2015), could lead to better utility. [truncated]","original_chars":1999},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Data and Preprocessing","heading_level":4,"line_start":58,"line_end":61,"text":"We use individual-level Variant Call Format (VCF) data of 2504 samples from the 1000 Genomes Project Phase 3 files (Auton et al. 2015). We focus on genetic mutations present on Chromosome 22 for all study samples in order to reduce computational overheads given its lower density of mutations, all while preserving the format and statistics of the data. To best allow the model to understand the underlying variants distributions in a sample-wise manner, we extracted individual genetic profiles from the source VCF file by querying the VCF dataset variant-wise (row-wise) and encoding each mutation as a string ' CHR : POS : REFs &gt; ALTs GT ' to introduce some context into the input, while keeping the amount of non-genomics related tokens to a minimum.","original_chars":757},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Methods","heading_level":3,"line_start":56,"line_end":71,"text":"#### Data and Preprocessing\n\nWe use individual-level Variant Call Format (VCF) data of 2504 samples from the 1000 Genomes Project Phase 3 files (Auton et al. 2015). We focus on genetic mutations present on Chromosome 22 for all study samples in order to reduce computational overheads given its lower density of mutations, all while preserving the format and statistics of the data. To best allow the model to understand the underlying variants distributions in a sample-wise manner, we extracted individual genetic profiles from the source VCF file by querying the VCF dataset variant-wise (row-wise) and encoding each mutation as a string ' CHR : POS : REFs &gt; ALTs GT ' to introduce some context into the input, while keeping the amount of non-genomics related tokens to a minimum.\n\n#### Models\n\nWe focused on generative models for Causal Language Modelling (CLM), as they learn to predict the next mutation in the sample profile based on the previously seen mutations, which we believed would allow for better contextualization and generation of biologically-plausible data. We finetuned OpenAI's GPT-2 Model (Radford et al. 2019) and trained an adaptation of MinGPT (Karpathy 2024b), a small, fast and interpretable reimplementation of the GPT-2 transformer that allows for full parameter and training customization. [truncated]","original_chars":4418}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_uncertain_no_vlm_2026-07-08/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_002329.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_002329","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary generative genomics paper using GPT-like models to generate synthetic mutation profiles. The record shows biological input/output but only biological mutation strings, not a substantive natural-language text component or text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"“generation of synthetic genetic mutation profiles”; mutations encoded as “CHR : POS : REFs > ALTs GT”; “GPT-like models are viable synthetic variant generators”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_002329","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The paper presents GPT-like language models for generating synthetic genomic mutation profiles, including causal language modeling and sequence generation. It does not clearly describe broad pretraining or other foundation-model-like characteristics beyond using GPT-2/MinGPT.","evidence_snippet":"\"generation of synthetic sample-level genomic mutation profiles\"; \"We focused on generative models for Causal Language Modelling (CLM)\"; \"GPT-like transformer-based models are valid generators\"; \"finetuned OpenAI's GPT-2\"; \"trained an adaptation of MinGPT\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}}]
